nges/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-3-usertest.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-4-scrutiny-v2.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-4-scrutiny.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-4-usertest.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-5-scrutiny.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-5-usertest.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-6-scrutiny.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-6-usertest.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-7-scrutiny.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-7-usertest.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-8-scrutiny.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-8-usertest.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/proposal.md\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/specs/\\n?? openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/tasks.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:43Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:45Z\",\"elapsed_s\":15,\"last_activity\":\"tool ended: command_execution\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The worktree already has many uncommitted and untracked mission files, so I’ll avoid touching anything outside this task’s files. Since this is a documentation artifact, I’ll use a small validation script as the “test” for the required frontmatter, links, and key workflow language.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/STACK.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/STACK.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/DEEP-COMPONENTS.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/DEEP-COMPONENTS.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/STACK.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Frontend Stack\\n\\nThis reference defines the default React frontend stack and conventions for Darkfactory frontend work.\\n\\n## Stack\\n\\n- React 19\\n- Vite\\n- TailwindCSS 4\\n- Radix UI / ShadCN\\n- Vitest\\n- React Testing Library\\n- React Router\\n\\n## Import Discipline\\n\\nUse direct imports only. Do not import UI components from barrel files.\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { Button } from '@/components/ui/button'\\nimport { cn } from '@/lib/utils'\\n```\\n\\nDo not use imports like this:\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { Button } from '@/components/ui'\\n```\\n\\nUse `cn()` from `@/lib/utils` for conditional class composition.\\n\\n## File Organization\\n\\n- `components/ui/` contains design system leaves such as buttons, badges, cards, fields, dialogs, and wrappers around Radix primitives.\\n- `components/<domain>/` contains deep components for a product or workflow area, such as `components/chat/` or `components/toyo/`.\\n- `hooks/` contains stateful logic extraction when the state belongs outside a single component or must be tested separately.\\n- `lib/` contains pure utilities with no React state and no rendering.\\n\\n## Semantic Color Tokens\\n\\nUse semantic color tokens such as `bg-card`, `text-card-foreground`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, and `bg-background`.\\n\\nDo not use raw Tailwind colors such as `bg-blue-500`, `text-zinc-600`, or `border-slate-200` in app components. Do not add manual `dark:` overrides. Theme behavior should come from tokens.\\n\\n## Data Slot Attributes\\n\\nFollow the ShadCN convention: each design system component renders a `data-slot` attribute for styling hooks.\\n\\n```tsx\\nfunction Card({ className, ...props }: React.ComponentProps<'div'>) {\\n  return (\\n    <div\\n      data-slot=\\\"card\\\"\\n      className={cn('bg-card text-card-foreground rounded-lg border', className)}\\n      {...props}\\n    />\\n  )\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Testing\\n\\nUse Vitest with React Testing Library. Test components through rendered behavior: roles, labels, visible text, user interaction, and final visible outcomes.\\n\\nDo not test implementation details such as hook calls, prop plumbing, internal state names, or class composition internals.\\n\\n## Routing\\n\\nUse React Router for client-side routing. Route modules should stay thin: read route params, load route-level data, and pass domain facts into deep feature components.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/DEEP-COMPONENTS.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Deep Components\\n\\nA deep component is a React component with a small semantic interface that hides meaningful state, effects, and rendering logic. It owns one user-facing workflow end-to-end. The caller should understand the domain concept and the final semantic events, not every intermediate step the component takes to render or coordinate that workflow.\\n\\nDepth is not about file length or raw prop count. It is about how much complexity the component removes from its consumers.\\n\\n## Four Rules\\n\\n### 1. Small Semantic Interfaces\\n\\nA component's prop interface should represent domain concepts, not implementation knobs. Count concepts across props, context providers, URL params, and cache dependencies. Do not count only raw props.\\n\\nA component with 6 props describing one concept can still be deep. A component with 1 prop bag containing 4 unrelated concerns is shallow because the caller must understand too many separate things.\\n\\nDiagnostic: \\\"How many independent domain concepts must a caller understand to use this component?\\\"\\n\\n### 2. Derive Don't Sync\\n\\nCompute values during render with inline computation or `useMemo` instead of syncing state with `useEffect`. Effects that transform data without I/O are a depth violation because they split one fact across two sources.\\n\\nUse effects for I/O, subscriptions, imperative browser APIs, and external systems. Do not use effects just to mirror props, filter arrays into state, or calculate display values. The canonical source is the React team's \\\"You Might Not Need an Effect.\\\"\\n\\nDiagnostic: \\\"Can this value be computed from existing state/props during render instead of stored separately?\\\"\\n\\n### 3. Composition Over Configuration\\n\\nUse compound component slots and children instead of config objects with many options. Callers should compose behavior without learning the child's internal option schema.\\n\\nA component accepting `items: [{ label, icon, onClick, disabled, variant }]` is shallower than one accepting `<Menu><MenuItem>...</MenuItem></Menu>` because the config shape turns component behavior into data plumbing. Prefer children and named slots when callers need to provide UI or behavior.\\n\\nDiagnostic: \\\"Would this be clearer as compound components with children/slots instead of a config prop?\\\"\\n\\n### 4. Collapse Coordination\\n\\nA deep component owns one user-facing workflow end-to-end. It exposes semantic events such as `onSubmit` and `onCancel`, not coordination knobs such as `onValidate`, `onStep1Complete`, or `onReset`.\\n\\nParents choose what happens when the workflow finishes or is cancelled. They should not drive every intermediate state inside the child. If the parent must choreograph validation, step transitions, reset behavior, and loading display, the component is not owning the workflow.\\n\\nDiagnostic: \\\"Does the parent coordinate state that belongs inside this component?\\\"\\n\\n## Allowed-Shallow Exceptions\\n\\n### Design System Leaves\\n\\n`Button`, `Badge`, `Card` sub-components, and similar UI leaves are thin by design. They should be small CVA wrappers with `data-slot` attributes. Their job is visual consistency and accessibility hooks, not workflow ownership.\\n\\n### Route/Layout Glue\\n\\n`App.tsx`, route modules, and page wrappers may read route params, load route-level data, and hand that data to a feature component. This is acceptable if they stay thin and do not become workflow owners.\\n\\n### Adapter Components\\n\\nComponents such as an auth callback page or OAuth redirect handler adapt external flows into the app. They are boundary concerns. They are allowed to be shallow because their purpose is integration, not behavior ownership.\\n\\n### Headless Behavior Primitives\\n\\nRadix Dialog, Combobox, Popover wrappers, and similar headless behavior primitives provide infrastructure without business rules. They may own accessibility and interaction mechanics, but they should not know domain logic.\\n\\n## Deletion Test\\n\\nUse this diagnostic before keeping a component:\\n\\n\\\"If you deleted this component, would complexity vanish (it was a pass-through) or reappear across every consumer (it was earning its keep)?\\\"\\n\\nIf deletion removes only a layer of prop forwarding, the component was shallow. If deletion forces every caller to recreate state, rendering branches, validation, and workflow steps, the component is deep.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/briefs/task-3.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/briefs/task-3.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'find openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/tests -maxdepth 1 -type f -print'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'find openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/tests -maxdepth 1 -type f -print'\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/briefs/task-3.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Worker Brief: Task 3 - Core /frontend SKILL.md\\n\\n## Your Task\\n\\nWrite the main `skills/frontend/SKILL.md` file — the core builder guide for React/TypeScript component development. This is a prescriptive, worker-facing skill that defines the design-implement-test workflow.\\n\\nThe file MUST start with this YAML frontmatter:\\n\\n```yaml\\n---\\nname: frontend\\nuser-invokable: true\\ndescription: Prescriptive builder guide for React/TypeScript component development. Use when building frontend UI components, pages, or layouts. Defines the design-implement-test workflow with visual verification. Not for hooks, utilities, or backend code — those use /tdd.\\n---\\n```\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nDefine a workflow with these phases, executed as a per-component loop:\\n\\n### Phase 1: Scan\\nInstruct the worker to:\\n- Search existing components in the feature area (grep for domain nouns in component file names)\\n- Identify reusable ShadCN/Radix primitives already in use (`grep -r \\\"@/components/ui\\\"`)\\n- Check existing hooks to consume (not write — hook creation is a separate /tdd task)\\n- Open 3-5 nearby components and their test files to learn patterns\\n- Check for existing dev routes, stories, or fixtures for visual verification\\n- Write a **scan note** listing: similar components found, reusable primitives, existing hooks, test pattern, visual mount point, constraints\\n\\n### Phase 2: Contract (per component, lightweight)\\nInstruct the worker to declare before building each component:\\n- **Component name**\\n- **Deep or allowed-shallow** classification with reason (if shallow)\\n- **Prop interface** with types — list each prop and what domain concept it represents\\n- **Key render states** — only those that apply: default, loading, error, empty, disabled\\n\\nThis is NOT a heavy artifact. Four items, written as a comment block or in the scan note.\\n\\n### Phase 3: Build (per component, with visual verification)\\nInstruct the worker to:\\n- Implement the component, composing from existing ShadCN/Radix primitives\\n- Apply the four deep-component rules from [DEEP-COMPONENTS.md](DEEP-COMPONENTS.md)\\n- Follow stack conventions from [STACK.md](STACK.md)\\n- If `browser_tools: true` in mission config: visually verify via chrome-devtools-mcp (navigate to the component, check layout, check render states)\\n- If `browser_tools: false` (human override): skip visual verification and proceed to Test\\n\\n### Phase 4: Test (per component)\\nInstruct the worker to:\\n- Write behavioral tests using Vitest + React Testing Library\\n- Follow testing guidance from [TESTING.md](TESTING.md)\\n- Test through rendered output: query by role, label, visible text; fire user events; assert visible outcomes\\n- Do NOT test implementation details (hook calls, internal state, prop plumbing, class names)\\n\\n### Per-Component Loop (forcing function)\\nState clearly: \\\"Complete Contract → Build → Test for each component before starting the next component. Do not build all components then test at the end.\\\"\\n\\n### Deep-Component Audit (before commit)\\nInclude a checklist the worker runs after all components are built and tested:\\n\\n```\\nBefore committing, review every .tsx file you created or modified:\\n[ ] Each prop represents a semantic domain concept, not an implementation knob\\n[ ] No useEffect syncing derived state (use useMemo or inline computation)\\n[ ] No config objects where compound components with slots would work\\n[ ] Parent doesn't coordinate state that belongs inside this component\\n[ ] Shallow components are classified as allowed-shallow with a reason\\n[ ] Behavioral tests cover key render states and user interactions\\n```\\n\\n## References\\n\\nInclude these markdown links in the file:\\n- `[DEEP-COMPONENTS.md](DEEP-COMPONENTS.md)` — Four rules and allowed-shallow exceptions\\n- `[STACK.md](STACK.md)` — Technology stack and conventions\\n- `[PATTERNS.md](PATTERNS.md)` — Concrete code examples (created in task 4)\\n- `[TESTING.md](TESTING.md)` — Frontend behavioral testing guidance (created in task 4)\\n\\n## Project Context\\n\\nThis skill is the frontend counterpart to `/tdd`. While `/tdd` uses red-green-refactor for code with testable interfaces, `/frontend` uses design-implement-test for React components where the output is visual and emergent.\\n\\n## Architecture\\n\\nThe file goes in `skills/frontend/SKILL.md` alongside the foundation files created in task 1 (DEEP-COMPONENTS.md, STACK.md). It will be referenced by the worker brief template (task 7) when a task has `Skill: /frontend`.\\n\\n## Key Terms\\n\\n- **Methodology**: /tdd (red-green-refactor) or /frontend (design-implement-test). Mutually exclusive per task.\\n- **Design-implement-test**: The /frontend workflow. Design component interfaces, build with visual verification, then write behavioral tests.\\n- **Component contract**: Lightweight per-component declaration (name, deep/shallow, props, render states) written before building.\\n- **Visual verification**: Using chrome-devtools-mcp during Build to confirm correct rendering.\\n- **Deep component**: A React component with small prop interface hiding significant behavior, owning one workflow.\\n- **Allowed-shallow exception**: Intentionally thin component exempt from depth rules.\\n\\n## Domain Types\\n\\nNone for this task.\\n\\n## What Previous Tasks Built\\n\\n### Task 1: Foundation vocabulary files\\n- Created: skills/frontend/DEEP-COMPONENTS.md, skills/frontend/STACK.md\\n- DEEP-COMPONENTS.md: four rules (Small Semantic Interfaces, Derive Don't Sync, Composition Over Configuration, Collapse Coordination), allowed-shallow exceptions (design system leaves, route/layout glue, adapters, headless primitives), deletion test\\n- STACK.md: React 19 + Vite + TailwindCSS 4 + Radix/ShadCN + Vitest + React Router, import discipline (no barrels, cn()), file organization, semantic tokens, data-slot\\n\\n### Task 2: Scrutiny validator gate 7\\n- Modified: skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md\\n- Inserted gate 7 (Frontend depth review) — self-skipping, checks four rules\\n- Renumbered gates 7-14 to 8-15 (now 15 gates total)\\n- Gate 1 now methodology-aware (\\\"/frontend Test phase not followed\\\")\\n\\n## Downstream Consumers\\n\\n- Task 4 (PATTERNS.md, TESTING.md) illustrates the workflow and rules defined here\\n- Task 7 (template modifications) references this skill in worker brief Procedures\\n- Task 8 (smoke test) verifies frontmatter and links\\n\\n## Procedures\\n\\n- Use `/tdd` to implement this task\\n- Commit all changes in a single commit with message: \\\"[task-3] Add core /frontend SKILL.md\\\"\\n- Write your handoff report to openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/handoffs/task-3.md\\n\\n## Handoff Report Format\\n\\n- Task: Task 3 - Core /frontend SKILL.md\\n- Commit SHA: <the full SHA>\\n- Approach: <brief explanation>\\n- Commands run: <any commands and their exit codes>\\n- Concerns: <anything uncertain>\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'find openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/tests -maxdepth 1 -type f -print'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/tests/test-task-1.sh\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:50Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I have the required frontmatter and the two existing reference docs now. I’m going to run a focused failing check for the missing `SKILL.md`, then add the guide and rerun stronger checks against the brief.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'test -f skills/frontend/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:57Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'test -f skills/frontend/SKILL.md'\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'test -f skills/frontend/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":1,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:39:57Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:40:00Z\",\"elapsed_s\":30,\"last_activity\":\"tool ended: command_execution\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’m adding the new worker-facing skill now. The content is deliberately prescriptive: scan first, define the small component contract, build with visual verification when enabled, then test that component before moving on.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:40:15Z\",\"elapsed_s\":45,\"last_activity\":\"agent response\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/stuart/.darkfactory/skills/frontend/SKILL.md\",\"kind\":\"add\"}],\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_start\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:40:29Z\",\"tool\":\"file_change\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/stuart/.darkfactory/skills/frontend/SKILL.md\",\"kind\":\"add\"}],\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:40:30Z\",\"elapsed_s\":60,\"last_activity\":\"tool started: file_change\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The initial doc is in place. I’m validating the exact frontmatter and the required workflow terms now, then I’ll add the handoff report with the real commit SHA after committing.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:40:40Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/SKILL.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' skills/frontend/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: frontend\\nuser-invokable: true\\ndescription: Prescriptive builder guide for React/TypeScript component development. Use when building frontend UI components, pages, or layouts. Defines the design-implement-test workflow with visual verification. Not for hooks, utilities, or backend code — those use /tdd.\\n---\\n\\n# Frontend Component Development\\n\\nUse this skill for React/TypeScript components, pages, and layouts. This is the frontend counterpart to `/tdd`: `/tdd` uses red-green-refactor for code with testable interfaces, while `/frontend` uses design-implement-test for visual React work where the final output emerges through component behavior, layout, and interaction.\\n\\nDo not use this skill for hooks, pure utilities, backend code, or non-rendering logic. Those belong in `/tdd`.\\n\\n## Method\\n\\nRun the workflow as a per-component loop:\\n\\n1. Scan the local frontend context.\\n2. Declare a lightweight component contract.\\n3. Build the component and visually verify it when browser tools are enabled.\\n4. Test rendered behavior.\\n\\nComplete Contract → Build → Test for each component before starting the next component. Do not build all components then test at the end.\\n\\n## Phase 1: Scan\\n\\nBefore writing component code, learn the local frontend shape.\\n\\n- Search existing components in the feature area. Grep for domain nouns in component file names.\\n- Identify reusable ShadCN/Radix primitives already in use with `grep -r \\\"@/components/ui\\\"`.\\n- Check existing hooks that should be consumed. Do not create hooks in a `/frontend` task; hook creation is a separate `/tdd` task.\\n- Open 3-5 nearby components and their test files to learn naming, layout, state handling, event patterns, and assertion style.\\n- Check for existing dev routes, stories, fixtures, or test harnesses that can mount the component for visual verification.\\n\\nWrite a scan note before building. Keep it short, but include:\\n\\n- Similar components found\\n- Reusable primitives\\n- Existing hooks to consume\\n- Test pattern\\n- Visual mount point\\n- Constraints\\n\\n## Phase 2: Contract\\n\\nBefore building each component, declare its contract. This is not a heavy artifact. Write the four items as a comment block near the work, in the scan note, or in the task log.\\n\\n- Component name\\n- Deep or allowed-shallow classification, with the reason if shallow\\n- Prop interface with types, listing each prop and the domain concept it represents\\n- Key render states that apply: default, loading, error, empty, disabled\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```tsx\\n/*\\nComponent: InvoiceReviewPanel\\nDepth: deep; owns the invoice review workflow from summary through submit/cancel.\\nProps:\\n- invoice: InvoiceReviewDraft - the invoice being reviewed\\n- reviewer: ReviewerIdentity - the active reviewer shown in review context\\n- onSubmit(decision: InvoiceDecision): void - final review outcome\\n- onCancel(): void - user exits review without a decision\\nRender states: default, loading, error, disabled\\n*/\\n```\\n\\n## Phase 3: Build\\n\\nImplement one component at a time.\\n\\n- Compose from existing ShadCN/Radix primitives before creating new UI leaves.\\n- Follow the four deep-component rules in [DEEP-COMPONENTS.md](DEEP-COMPONENTS.md): Small Semantic Interfaces, Derive Don't Sync, Composition Over Configuration, and Collapse Coordination.\\n- Follow stack conventions in [STACK.md](STACK.md), including direct imports, `cn()`, semantic tokens, `data-slot`, and local file organization.\\n- Use [PATTERNS.md](PATTERNS.md) for concrete examples when it exists.\\n- Keep route and layout glue thin. Push meaningful workflow ownership into the deep feature component.\\n- If new state is derived from props or other state, compute it inline or with `useMemo`. Do not sync derived state with `useEffect`.\\n- If a component needs stateful logic that should live outside the component, stop and split that hook into a `/tdd` task instead of hiding hook creation inside `/frontend`.\\n\\n### Visual Verification\\n\\nIf `browser_tools: true` in mission config, visually verify during the Build phase with `chrome-devtools-mcp`.\\n\\n- Navigate to the dev route, story, fixture, or harness that mounts the component.\\n- Check the default layout at realistic viewport sizes.\\n- Check each relevant render state from the contract: loading, error, empty, and disabled.\\n- Exercise the main interaction path enough to see the resulting UI state.\\n- Fix visible layout, overflow, spacing, focus, and state issues before moving to Test.\\n\\nIf `browser_tools: false` in mission config, or the human has explicitly overridden browser use, skip visual verification and proceed to Test. Note the skip in the handoff.\\n\\n## Phase 4: Test\\n\\nWrite behavioral tests for the component using Vitest and React Testing Library. Follow [TESTING.md](TESTING.md) for detailed frontend testing guidance.\\n\\nTest through rendered output:\\n\\n- Query by role, label, and visible text.\\n- Fire user events that match real interaction.\\n- Assert visible outcomes and semantic events.\\n- Cover the key render states from the component contract.\\n\\nDo not test implementation details:\\n\\n- No assertions on hook calls\\n- No assertions on internal state names\\n- No prop plumbing tests\\n- No class name or Tailwind implementation assertions\\n- No tests that only prove a child component was passed a value\\n\\nTests should describe what a user can see or do. They should survive internal refactors that preserve behavior.\\n\\n## Deep-Component Audit\\n\\nBefore committing, review every `.tsx` file you created or modified:\\n\\n```text\\nBefore committing, review every .tsx file you created or modified:\\n[ ] Each prop represents a semantic domain concept, not an implementation knob\\n[ ] No useEffect syncing derived state (use useMemo or inline computation)\\n[ ] No config objects where compound components with slots would work\\n[ ] Parent doesn't coordinate state that belongs inside this component\\n[ ] Shallow components are classified as allowed-shallow with a reason\\n[ ] Behavioral tests cover key render states and user interactions\\n```\\n\\nIf a component fails the audit, fix it before committing. If it is intentionally shallow, record the allowed-shallow reason from [DEEP-COMPONENTS.md](DEEP-COMPONENTS.md).\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- [DEEP-COMPONENTS.md](DEEP-COMPONENTS.md) - Four rules and allowed-shallow exceptions\\n- [STACK.md](STACK.md) - Technology stack and conventions\\n- [PATTERNS.md](PATTERNS.md) - Concrete code examples\\n- [TESTING.md](TESTING.md) - Frontend behavioral testing guidance\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR3C6N264EWQCJQTKS3NG3D\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-03T23:40:40Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"diff -u \\\\\\\\\\n  <(printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' \\\\\\\\\\n    '---' \\\\\\\\\\n    'name: frontend' \\\\\\\\\\n    'user-invokable: