\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' /Users/stuart/.darkfactory/skills/tdd/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: tdd\\nuser-invokable: true\\ndescription: Test-driven development with red-green-refactor loop. Use when user wants to build features or fix bugs using TDD, mentions \\\"red-green-refactor\\\", wants integration tests, or asks for test-first development.\\n---\\n\\n# Test-Driven Development\\n\\n## Philosophy\\n\\n**Core principle**: Tests should verify behavior through public interfaces, not implementation details. Code can change entirely; tests shouldn't.\\n\\n**Good tests** are integration-style: they exercise real code paths through public APIs. They describe _what_ the system does, not _how_ it does it. A good test reads like a specification - \\\"user can checkout with valid cart\\\" tells you exactly what capability exists. These tests survive refactors because they don't care about internal structure.\\n\\n**Bad tests** are coupled to implementation. They mock internal collaborators, test private methods, or verify through external means (like querying a database directly instead of using the interface). The warning sign: your test breaks when you refactor, but behavior hasn't changed. If you rename an internal function and tests fail, those tests were testing implementation, not behavior.\\n\\nSee [tests.md](tests.md) for examples and [mocking.md](mocking.md) for mocking guidelines.\\n\\n## Anti-Pattern: Horizontal Slices\\n\\n**DO NOT write all tests first, then all implementation.** This is \\\"horizontal slicing\\\" - treating RED as \\\"write all tests\\\" and GREEN as \\\"write all code.\\\"\\n\\nThis produces **crap tests**:\\n\\n- Tests written in bulk test _imagined_ behavior, not _actual_ behavior\\n- You end up testing the _shape_ of things (data structures, function signatures) rather than user-facing behavior\\n- Tests become insensitive to real changes - they pass when behavior breaks, fail when behavior is fine\\n- You outrun your headlights, committing to test structure before understanding the implementation\\n\\n**Correct approach**: Vertical slices via tracer bullets. One test → one implementation → repeat. Each test responds to what you learned from the previous cycle. Because you just wrote the code, you know exactly what behavior matters and how to verify it.\\n\\n```\\nWRONG (horizontal):\\n  RED:   test1, test2, test3, test4, test5\\n  GREEN: impl1, impl2, impl3, impl4, impl5\\n\\nRIGHT (vertical):\\n  RED→GREEN: test1→impl1\\n  RED→GREEN: test2→impl2\\n  RED→GREEN: test3→impl3\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### 1. Planning\\n\\nBefore writing any code:\\n\\n- [ ] Confirm with user what interface changes are needed\\n- [ ] Confirm with user which behaviors to test (prioritize)\\n- [ ] Identify opportunities for [deep modules](deep-modules.md) (small interface, deep implementation)\\n- [ ] Design interfaces for [testability](interface-design.md)\\n- [ ] List the behaviors to test (not implementation steps)\\n- [ ] Get user approval on the plan\\n\\nAsk: \\\"What should the public interface look like? Which behaviors are most important to test?\\\"\\n\\n**You can't test everything.** Confirm with the user exactly which behaviors matter most. Focus testing effort on critical paths and complex logic, not every possible edge case.\\n\\n### 2. Tracer Bullet\\n\\nWrite ONE test that confirms ONE thing about the system:\\n\\n```\\nRED:   Write test for first behavior → test fails\\nGREEN: Write minimal code to pass → test passes\\n```\\n\\nThis is your tracer bullet - proves the path works end-to-end.\\n\\n### 3. Incremental Loop\\n\\nFor each remaining behavior:\\n\\n```\\nRED:   Write next test → fails\\nGREEN: Minimal code to pass → passes\\n```\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n- One test at a time\\n- Only enough code to pass current test\\n- Don't anticipate future tests\\n- Keep tests focused on observable behavior\\n\\n### 4. Refactor\\n\\nAfter all tests pass, look for [refactor candidates](refactoring.md):\\n\\n- [ ] Extract duplication\\n- [ ] Deepen modules (move complexity behind simple interfaces)\\n- [ ] Apply SOLID principles where natural\\n- [ ] Consider what new code reveals about existing code\\n- [ ] Run tests after each refactor step\\n\\n**Never refactor while RED.** Get to GREEN first.\\n\\n## Checklist Per Cycle\\n\\n```\\n[ ] Test describes behavior, not implementation\\n[ ] Test uses public interface only\\n[ ] Test would survive internal refactor\\n[ ] Code is minimal for this test\\n[ ] No speculative features added\\n```\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR4JYYSZS01CS01QFAKE5E0\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-04T00:00:52Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR4JYYSZS01CS01QFAKE5E0\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-04T00:00:55Z\",\"elapsed_s\":15,\"last_activity\":\"tool ended: command_execution\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The `/tdd` workflow is test-first, but this task is documentation/template wiring with no runtime interface. I’ll treat verification as diff-based checks plus targeted greps after the patch, rather than adding tests that would only mirror markdown text.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' skills/mission/templates/worker-brief.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR4JYYSZS01CS01QFAKE5E0\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-04T00:00:59Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' skills/mission/templates/worker-brief.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' skills/mission/templates/worker-brief.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Worker Brief: Task N - <name>\\n\\n## Your Task\\n<task description from tasks.md, expanded with acceptance criteria>\\n\\n## Project Context\\n<relevant excerpt from proposal.md -- what is being built and why>\\n\\n## Architecture\\n<relevant excerpt from design.md -- how this task fits into the system>\\n\\n## Key Terms (from UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md)\\n<any domain terms the worker needs to understand>\\n\\n## Domain Types\\nUse the exact Type Catalog names listed here when modeling domain concepts. Include entries relevant to this task from both the Produces and Consumes topology fields.\\n\\n<For each relevant Type Catalog entry, include:\\n- TypeName: one-line purpose and owning module\\n\\nIf no Type Catalog entries are relevant, write:\\nNone for this task.\\n>\\n\\n## What Previous Tasks Built\\n<For task 1, this section is empty: \\\"This is the first task. No prior code has been committed for this mission.\\\"\\n\\nFor task 2+, include a codebase delta summary generated from `git diff` after each previous task passed validation. List ONLY observable facts about the code -- never include worker reasoning, handoff content, or concerns.\\n\\nFor each completed task, include:\\n- Files created or modified (paths only)\\n- Public function/endpoint signatures added (name, params, return type)\\n- Key type definitions or interfaces introduced\\n- Patterns used (e.g., \\\"repository pattern via src/repositories/userRepo.ts\\\")\\n\\nExample:\\n### Task 1: User service\\n- Created: src/services/users.ts, src/models/user.ts, src/repositories/userRepo.ts\\n- Endpoints: POST /api/users (body: {email: string, name: string}) → 201 {id, email, name}\\n- Types: User {id: string, email: string, name: string, createdAt: Date}\\n- Pattern: repository pattern via src/repositories/userRepo.ts\\n\\n### Task 2: Auth middleware\\n- Created: src/middleware/requireAuth.ts, src/services/auth.ts\\n- Exports: requireAuth (Express middleware, attaches req.user: User)\\n- Endpoints: POST /api/auth/login (body: {email, password}) → 200 {token: string}\\n>\\n\\n## Downstream Consumers\\n<Which later tasks in tasks.md will call this code? For each, name one minimum integration test that proves the call path works. Leave empty if no later task consumes this one.>\\n\\n## Procedures\\n- Use `/tdd` to implement this task\\n- Commit all changes (code + tests) in a single commit with message: \\\"[task-N] <description>\\\"\\n- Write your handoff report to openspec/changes/<change-name>/mission/handoffs/task-N.md\\n\\n## Handoff Report Format\\n- Task: Task N - <name>\\n- Commit SHA: <the full SHA>\\n- Approach: <brief explanation of what you did and why>\\n- Commands run: <any commands and their exit codes>\\n- Concerns: <anything you're uncertain about>\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR4JYYSZS01CS01QFAKE5E0\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-04T00:00:59Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,130p' skills/mission/templates/design-template.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR4JYYSZS01CS01QFAKE5E0\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-04T00:00:59Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,130p' skills/mission/templates/design-template.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,130p' skills/mission/templates/design-template.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Design Template\\n\\nUse this template for every `design.md`. Include all six mandatory sections below.\\n\\n## Architecture Decision\\n\\nFor greenfield missions or missions with explicit architecture changes, evaluate 2-3 architecture candidates. Score each candidate on every dimension from 0-3, then recommend one candidate.\\n\\nFor existing codebase missions without architecture changes, document the current architecture and explain how the planned changes fit. Scored candidates are not required.\\n\\n| Candidate | Separation of concerns | Testability | Task independence | Module depth | Total | Recommendation |\\n|-----------|------------------------|-------------|-------------------|--------------|-------|----------------|\\n| Candidate A | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\\n| Candidate B | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\\n\\n### Scoring Rubric\\n\\n#### Separation of Concerns\\n\\n| Score | Criteria |\\n|-------|----------|\\n| 3 | Each responsibility maps to exactly one unit. No unit mixes domain logic with transport, storage, or configuration. |\\n| 2 | Mostly isolated but 1-2 units mix concerns pragmatically. |\\n| 1 | Several units mix concerns. Domain logic tangled with transport or storage in more than one place. |\\n| 0 | No clear separation. Business logic, I/O, and configuration interleaved throughout. |\\n\\n#### Testability\\n\\n| Score | Criteria |\\n|-------|----------|\\n| 3 | Each module testable in isolation with swappable dependencies via interfaces or dependency injection. |\\n| 2 | Most modules testable in isolation. One or two require a real external dependency. |\\n| 1 | Tests require significant setup or multiple running services. Modules tightly coupled to dependencies. |\\n| 0 | No practical way to test modules in isolation. Testing requires the full system running. |\\n\\n#### Task Independence\\n\\n| Score | Criteria |\\n|-------|----------|\\n| 3 | Separately buildable or deployable units. A task can modify one unit without touching others. |\\n| 2 | Explicit interface boundaries within a single build unit with enforced contracts. |\\n| 1 | Directory/namespace separation only. No enforcement beyond developer discipline. |\\n| 0 | Flat structure. No meaningful boundaries. Every task touches shared state. |\\n\\n#### Module Depth\\n\\n| Score | Criteria |\\n|-------|----------|\\n| 3 | Modules expose small public API relative to implementation. Callers don't need to understand internals. |\\n| 2 | Reasonable interface-to-implementation ratio. One or two modules expose more surface than they hide. |\\n| 1 | Several modules are thin wrappers. Public API nearly as large as implementation. |\\n| 0 | No meaningful abstraction. Public surface and implementation effectively the same. |\\n\\nAfter the table, include a short rationale for the recommended architecture. If candidates tie on total score, present the tied candidates and their tradeoffs for human selection.\\n\\n## Module Inventory\\n\\nList each planned module or major component. Use this table format:\\n\\n| Module name | Interface width | What it hides | Why this boundary |\\n|-------------|-----------------|---------------|-------------------|\\n| `<module>` | Narrow / moderate / broad, with the public entry points named | Implementation details callers do not need to know | Reason this responsibility belongs behind this boundary |\\n\\n## Type Catalog\\n\\nList domain types only. Type names must come from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` when the term exists there. Do not include field-level detail.\\nDomain types model the problem space; exclude technical helpers such as CLI parsers, test fixtures, and generic containers unless they are public compatibility surfaces.\\n\\n| Type name | Purpose | Owning module |\\n|-----------|---------|---------------|\\n| `<type>` | One-line purpose | `<module>` |\\n\\n## Error Strategy\\n\\nDefine:\\n- Error type hierarchy\\n- Propagation rules\\n- Which layer throws each error category\\n- Which layer catches and translates each error category\\n\\nKeep this at the design level. Do not list every possible error instance unless the specs require it.\\n\\n## Integration Points\\n\\nDescribe how modules call each other. Use this table format:\\n\\n| Caller | Callee | Method/interface | Direction |\\n|--------|--------|------------------|-----------|\\n| `<module>` | `<module>` | Function, command, event, adapter, or interface name | Inbound, outbound, or internal |\\n\\n## Test Strategy\\n\\nDefine:\\n- Test framework and runner\\n- Where tests live (colocated, separate directory, by module)\\n- Which modules get unit tests vs integration tests and why\\n- What needs real external dependencies (database, network) vs mocks/stubs\\n- Any special setup (serial execution, test database, environment variables)\\n- Coverage approach (which code paths are critical to cover, which are low-value)\\n\\nKeep it brief. This section captures decisions that affect every worker, not a full test plan.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR4JYYSZS01CS01QFAKE5E0\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-04T00:00:59Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQR4JYYSZS01CS01QFAKE5E0\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-04T00:00:59Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short'\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short'\",\"aggregated_output\":\" M UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/.openspec.yaml\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/contract.md\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/coverage-audit.md\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/design.md\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/briefs/\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/config.yaml\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/handoffs/\\n?? openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/logs/\\n?? openspec/changes/