te:** Run `git show <commit-sha> --stat` and check if any test files were added or modified. If the diff contains zero test files [... omitted end of long line]\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:182:10. **Contract assertions:** Mark each assertion PASS, FAIL, or SKIP with a one-line explanation. SKIPs for work deferred to later tasks: append to `m [... omitted end of long line]\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:184:11. **Behavioral conformance:** For each assertion that describes observable behavior (CLI output, exit codes, file content, API response, shell behav [... omitted end of long line]\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:185:    - CLI flag assertion: run the binary with the flag, check output\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:186:    - Exit code assertion: run the command, check `$?`\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:187:    - Shell alias assertion: source the alias, run `type <name>`, check the definition\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:188:    - API response assertion: curl the endpoint, check the response body\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:190:    If an assertion cannot be tested via execution (e.g., internal architecture, code organization, build configuration), mark it as \\\"verified by insp [... omitted end of long line]\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:194:    Report each assertion's verification method (execution or inspection) in the contract assertions table.\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:251:    - Any P0 finding, including tautological assertions, is a FAIL.\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:257:    Report passing output as `test-quality | PASS | no findings`. Report warning output as `test-quality | WARN | 2 P2, 1 P3 findings`. Report failing [... omitted end of long line]\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:282:| Gate 10: Contract assertions | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:288:## Contract Assertions\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:289:| # | Assertion | Result | Method | Evidence |\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:291:| 1 | <assertion text> | PASS/FAIL | execution/inspection | <one-line evidence> |\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:294:## Failed Assertions (if any)\\nskills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md:301:IMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY gate fails or ANY contract assertion fails. Be strict. A near-miss is still a FAIL.\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:37:1. **Scrutiny validator** -- test presence gate, build/test/lint, /review, code standards, /simplify, /security (conditional), contract assertions. Al [... omitted end of long line]\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:44:Validators receive: commit SHA + contract assertions. They do NOT receive proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:84:- contract.md is a new OpenSpec artifact (validation assertions, written during planning)\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:140:5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:142:7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:219:- [Assertion verifiable from outside without knowing the code]\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:220:- [Another assertion]\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:223:- [Assertion]\\ndocs/design-decisions.md:232:[Specific failed assertions from the validator report]\\ndocs/reference_factory_talk.md:21:- **Orchestrators** -- plan features/milestones, ask clarifying questions, produce a \\\"validation contract\\\" (defines done before coding starts, can be  [... omitted end of long line]\\ndocs/provenance.md:18:| Validation contract | \\\"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions\\\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but befo [... omitted end of long line]\\ndocs/provenance.md:22:| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build [... omitted end of long line]\\ndocs/provenance.md:69:| Contract cross-referencing to PRD | Assertions trace to specific PRD requirements. Deviated items are marked. |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:02Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:02Z\",\"elapsed_s\":15,\"last_activity\":\"tool ended: command_execution\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The initial scan shows the expected contract-language hits plus the two test-quality phrases that should remain. I’m reading the nearby context now so the edits stay limited to contract items.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"You are a user-test validator for the Darkfactory mission system. Your job is to verify that a worker's commit delivers the correct user-facing behavior. You have NOT seen the proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\\n\\n## Commit to Validate\\nSHA: <commit-sha>\\n\\n## Contract Assertions for This Task\\n<paste the assertions from contract.md for task N>\\n\\n## Instructions\\n\\n1. **Read the diff:** Run `git show <commit-sha>` to understand what changed.\\n\\n2. **Decide your test strategy** based on what the diff touches:\\n   - **Backend only** (API routes, database, server logic, CLI): Write and run HTTP tests against the API endpoints. Use curl or a test script.\\n   - **Frontend only** (UI components, pages, styles): Run /test-app to drive a browser and verify UI behavior.\\n   - **Both backend and frontend**: Run HTTP tests first, then /test-app for the UI.\\n   - **Infrastructure only** (config, build, CI): Verify the build passes and config is correct. You may mark behavioral assertions as SKIP with reason \\\"infrastructure-only change, not testable via HTTP or browser.\\\"\\n\\n3. **Check each contract assertion:** For each assertion, run the appropriate test. Mark PASS, FAIL, or SKIP.\\n   - PASS: The assertion is demonstrably true.\\n   - FAIL: The assertion is demonstrably false, or the test reveals a bug.\\n   - SKIP: The assertion cannot be tested from outside (e.g., internal refactor) or the work is deferred to a later task. Provide a reason. If deferred to a later task, append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <assertion>`.\\n\\n## Output Format\\n\\nWrite your report to: <absolute path to mission/validations/task-N-usertest.md>\\n\\nUse this format:\\n\\n```markdown\\n# User-Test Validation: Task N\\n\\n## Result: PASS | FAIL\\n\\n## Test Strategy\\n<what you decided and why>\\n\\n## Tests Run\\n| # | Test Description | Method | Result |\\n|---|-----------------|--------|--------|\\n| 1 | <what was tested> | HTTP/Browser/Manual | PASS/FAIL |\\n| 2 | ... | ... | ... |\\n\\n## Contract Assertions\\n| # | Assertion | Result | Evidence |\\n|---|-----------|--------|----------|\\n| 1 | <assertion text> | PASS/FAIL/SKIP | <evidence or skip reason> |\\n| 2 | ... | ... | ... |\\n\\n## Failed Assertions (if any)\\n<detailed explanation of each failure>\\n\\n## Recommendations (if FAIL)\\n<specific guidance for fixing the failures>\\n```\\n\\nIMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY contract assertion fails (SKIP does not count as failure). Be thorough -- actually run the tests, don't just read the code and guess.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '50,90p' skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '50,90p' skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,330p' skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,330p' skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '50,90p' skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"2. **If the commit is small** (say, under ~600 changed lines and no single file over ~400 lines): read `git show <sha>` in one shot and review it.\\n3. **If the commit is large or touches generated/lock/vendor files:** skip generated/lock/vendor files (`package-lock.json`, `yarn.lock`, `*.min.js`, `dist/**`, `vendor/**`, `__snapshots__/**`, etc.) and iterate the remaining files with `git show <sha> -- <path>` one file at a time. Review each, dropping the diff text between files.\\n4. Review against the contract and the adversarial checklist (below).\\n5. Append to the `## Task N` section of the report:\\n   - A short **Surface summary** listing changed files and any exported symbols/APIs/types/flags introduced or modified. Keep it compact (one line per file). This is the signal the cross-task pass will use.\\n   - A findings table with severity, file:line, and description.\\n   Fix commits append under the same task as the original commit -- do not create separate sections for them.\\n6. **Drop the diff text from your working context before moving to the next commit.** You only need to carry forward a short list of findings so far, not the raw diffs.\\n\\n### Adversarial checklist (apply per commit)\\n\\n- Logic errors or edge cases not covered by tests\\n- Cross-file inconsistencies within this commit (types, interfaces, naming)\\n- Contract assertions that technically pass but have subtle flaws\\n- Security issues, race conditions, or data integrity problems\\n- Missing error handling or graceful degradation\\n- Test doubles leaking into production code paths (NullBridge pattern)\\n- Gaming of contract assertions (smoke test that bypasses real wiring, etc.)\\n\\nFor each finding, classify severity as HIGH, MEDIUM, or LOW. Cite file paths and line numbers.\\n\\n## Step 3: Cross-task pass\\n\\nOnce every commit has been reviewed, re-read the `## Task N` sections of your own report -- both the surface summaries and the findings. Do not re-read the raw diffs. Use the surface summaries to spot interactions the per-commit pass cannot see:\\n\\n- A type or interface introduced in task N consumed incorrectly by task N+k\\n- Race conditions or ordering issues between tasks touching shared state\\n- A contract assertion that no single commit falsifies but the combined behavior does\\n- Integration gaps where task A's outputs do not meet task B's expectations\\n\\nTo verify suspicions, read the **final state** of files from the current working tree (not historical commit blobs). The working tree reflects what will actually ship; commit blobs reflect a point in time that may have been amended by a later fix. Append findings to the `## Cross-task` section.\\n\\n## Step 4: Summary\\n\\nWrite a one-paragraph `## Summary` covering the overall posture of the mission: counts of findings by severity, the biggest risk you see, and whether you would recommend shipping as-is.\\n\\n## Output Format\\n\\nWrite the report to the Report Path. Structure:\\n\\n```markdown\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,330p' skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"You are a scrutiny validator for the Darkfactory mission system. Your job is to verify that a worker's commit meets the validation contract. You have NOT seen the proposal, design, or worker reasoning. You judge only by the code and the contract.\\n\\n## Commit to Validate\\nSHA: <commit-sha>\\n\\n## Contract Assertions for This Task\\n<paste the assertions from contract.md for task N>\\n\\n## Deterministic Pre-passes\\n\\nBefore starting the gate sequence, gather deterministic facts from the repository and the task diff. Store the results under the exact named facts below so later gates can reference them by name. Do not inline these facts into individual gate descriptions.\\n\\n### Named facts\\n\\n- `Detected language: <Rust | TypeScript/Node | Python | Unknown>`\\n- `File sizes: <filename>: <lines>, <filename>: <lines>, ...`\\n- `Error handling smells: <count> matches in <lines> changed production lines (<N> per KLOC). Locations: <file:line> <pattern>, ...`\\n- `Test runner: <passed> passed, <failed> failed, <skipped> skipped (<pass_rate>% pass rate)`\\n\\n### 1. Language detection\\n\\nDetect the project's primary language from build files at the repo root:\\n\\n- `Cargo.toml` -> Rust\\n- `package.json` -> TypeScript/Node\\n- `pyproject.toml` or `requirements.txt` -> Python\\n\\nIf more than one build file type exists, choose the language with the most touched production source files in the task diff. If no recognized build file exists, store `Detected language: Unknown` and skip language-specific pre-passes with a note.\\n\\n### 2. File size count\\n\\nRun `git show <commit-sha> --stat` to identify every file the task touched. For the detected language, count lines with `wc -l` on touched source files:\\n\\n- Rust: `.rs`\\n- TypeScript/Node: `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`, `.jsx`\\n- Python: `.py`\\n\\nExclude standalone test files from this count:\\n\\n- Rust: files in `tests/`\\n- TypeScript/Node: `*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`, files in `__tests__/`\\n- Python: `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, files in `tests/`\\n\\nDo not exclude test modules embedded in production files. `#[cfg(test)]` blocks inside production Rust files count toward the line total.\\n\\nStore the result as `File sizes: <filename>: <lines>, <filename>: <lines>, ...`. If no touched production source files match the detected language, store `File sizes: none`.\\n\\n### 3. Error handling smell grep\\n\\nCount only added production lines from `git diff <commit-sha>^ <commit-sha>`, excluding standalone test files using the same language-specific rules as the file size count. Record each match with the new-file path and line number.\\n\\nFlag these Rust patterns:\\n\\n- `.unwrap()`\\n- `panic!()`\\n- `todo!()`\\n- `unimplemented!()`\\n- `.expect(\\\"\\\")`\\n\\nDo not flag these Rust safe patterns:\\n\\n- `.unwrap_or()`\\n- `.unwrap_or_default()`\\n- `.unwrap_or_else()`\\n- `.expect(\\\"non-empty message\\\")`\\n\\nFlag these TypeScript/Node patterns:\\n\\n- `catch {}`\\n- `catch (e) {}`\\n- `as any`\\n- `// @ts-ignore`\\n- `// @ts-expect-error` without explanation\\n- `.catch(() => {})`\\n\\nDo not flag these TypeScript/Node safe patterns:\\n\\n- `catch` with a handler body\\n- `as unknown`\\n\\nFlag these Python patterns:\\n\\n- `except: pass`\\n- `except Exception: pass`\\n- `type: ignore` without a specific code\\n- bare `raise` without context\\n\\nDo not flag this Python safe pattern:\\n\\n- `except SpecificError:` with a handler\\n\\nCompute density as `count / (changed_production_lines / 1000)`. Report `0 per KLOC` when there are zero changed production lines.\\n\\nStore the result as `Error handling smells: <count> matches in <lines> changed production lines (<N> per KLOC). Locations: <file:line> <pattern>, ...`. If there are no matches, use `Locations: none`.\\n\\n### 4. Test runner output parsing\\n\\nThis named fact is declared before the gate sequence, then filled immediately after gate 3 runs the project test command. Parse the captured test stdout/stderr summary line using the detected language:\\n\\n- Rust: `test result: ok. N passed; M failed; P ignored`\\n- Jest: `Tests: N passed, M failed, P skipped, Q total`\\n- Vitest uses the same `Tests:` summary shape as Jest\\n- Pytest: `N passed, M failed, P skipped`\\n\\nTreat Rust `ignored` as skipped for the named fact. Compute pass rate as `passed / (passed + failed + skipped) * 100`, rounded to one decimal place. If no summary line is found, store `Test runner: unavailable`.\\n\\nStore the result as `Test runner: <passed> passed, <failed> failed, <skipped> skipped (<pass_rate>% pass rate)`.\\n\\n## Gate Sequence\\n\\nRun these gates in order. For each, report PASS or FAIL with evidence.\\n\\n1. **Test presence gate:** Run `git show <commit-sha> --stat` and check if any test files were added or modified. If the diff contains zero test files and zero new test assertions, this is an automatic FAIL. Workers are required to use /tdd -- no new tests means TDD was not followed. Exception: closing tasks that produce no testable code (documentation, cleanup, module shape re-check) are exempt from this gate.\\n\\n2. **Build:** Run the project's build command. Does it pass?\\n\\n3. **Tests:** Run the project's test command. Capture stdout/stderr and the exit code. After the command finishes, fill the `Test runner` named fact by parsing the captured summary line as described in the deterministic pre-pass.\\n\\n   Report the Tests row using structured counts from `Test runner`, for example `tests | FAIL | 49 passed, 46 failed, 2 ignored (51.5% pass rate)` or `tests | PASS | 234 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored (100% pass rate)`. If the parser stored `skipped`, use the test runner's native term in the report (`ignored` for Rust, `skipped` for Jest/Vitest/Pytest). If no summary line is found, report `Test runner: unavailable` and include the raw command exit status plus the most relevant summary text you can find.\\n\\n   If the test command exits non-zero, mark Tests as FAIL. If tests fail, group failures that share the same error message or failure signature. Include the group count and likely cause when the output makes it clear, for example: `46 failures in 1 group: 'connection refused to 127.0.0.1:5432' -- infrastructure-dependent tests running without infrastructure.`\\n\\n   If the test command exits 0 but fewer than 90% of discovered tests actually ran, keep Tests as PASS and add a WARNING note: `Only N of M tests actually ran. The rest were skipped/ignored.` Actual run rate is `(passed + failed) / (passed + failed + skipped_or_ignored) * 100`.\\n\\n4. **Lint:** Run the project's lint command. Are there any violations?\\n\\n5. **Code review:** Run /review against the diff (this includes reading the full diff). Check specifically for:\\n   - Bugs: off-by-one errors, null/undefined access, race conditions, missing error handling at boundaries\\n   - Security: injection, auth issues, secrets in code, unsafe input handling\\n   - Logic: wrong conditions, missing edge cases, incorrect assumptions\\n   - Breaking changes: API contract changes, removed exports, changed behavior\\n   Report findings by severity (critical / warning / nit) with file:line references.\\n\\n6. **Module depth review:** Evaluate whether the PR changes module depth in a healthy direction. Deep modules (narrow interface, rich hidden behaviour) are the design ideal. Shallow modules (wide interface relative to what they hide) are the design liability.\\n\\n   Before evaluating module depth, read the `File sizes` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass.\\n\\n   File size context:\\n   - If any touched production source file in `File sizes` exceeds 900 lines, name that file and its line count in the module depth evaluation.\\n   - Cross-reference `design.md` for concern boundaries for that module.\\n   - If `design.md` specifies concern boundaries that the implementation did not follow, flag Module depth as FAIL.\\n   - If `design.md` says this module is intentionally one file because of a specific reason, accept that file size as PASS.\\n   - If `design.md` does not address concern boundaries for that module, flag Module depth as FAIL for a design gap.\\n   - Exclude standalone test files from the 900-line check: files in `tests/` directories, `*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`, `test_*.py`, and `*_test.py`.\\n   - Rust `#[cfg(test)]` blocks inside production files count toward the 900-line threshold.\\n\\n   Flag any of the following:\\n\\n   - **Interface widening:** A module gains new public methods, exported symbols, or required parameters without a commensurate increase in hidden complexity. Each new surface point is a coupling tax on every caller; justify it. If removing the new export would force callers to duplicate 3+ branches or non-inlineable control flow, it is deep; if callers could inline it in one line, it is shallow. Facade and adapter modules, and idiomatic or framework-required patterns where thin delegation is the convention — for example, TypeScript barrel re-exports (`index.ts`), Python `__init__.py` re-exports, Next.js route handlers, or Rust trait impls — are not shallow-module violations.\\n   - **Information leaking:** Implementation details — internal types, error codes, processing order, config shape — surface in the public interface or in caller code that now has to know them.\\n   - **Premature splitting:** One coherent abstraction is divided into two or more shallower modules whose combined interface is wider than the original and whose separation does not reflect a genuine domain boundary. If the diff created files A, B, and C and none of them has independent callers outside the group, they should be one module.\\n   - **Overgrown shallow module:** A module that was already shallow accumulates more interface without gaining proportional depth.\\n   - **Deep module preserved or deepened (safe harbour):** The change keeps the interface stable or narrows it while adding or consolidating hidden behaviour. This is the target state; note it briefly and move on. Do not flag large generated files, schemas, fixtures, localization data, snapshots, SVG/assets, lockfiles, or test data as module-depth failures.\\n\\n   Verdict guidance:\\n   - PASS when any interface addition is minor, justified by hidden complexity, or required by an idiomatic/framework pattern; when module boundaries stay stable or become narrower; and when any split modules have independent callers or clear domain boundaries.\\n   - FAIL when the diff creates systematic interface widening, leaks implementation details into public interfaces or callers, splits one coherent abstraction into files without independent callers or domain boundaries, or adds surface area to an already shallow module without proportional hidden complexity.\\n\\n   Report: write one bullet per individual finding instance; state which symbol or boundary is affected and why it matters for depth. If nothing flagged, write 'No module depth issues.'\\n\\n7. **Code standards gate:** Review the diff for these specific anti-patterns. Flag any found:\\n   Before reviewing code standards, read the `Error handling smells` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass.\\n\\n   Error handling smell density:\\n   - Use the smell count, changed production line count, per-KLOC density, and locations in the Code standards result.\\n   - Under 5 per KLOC: PASS for the error handling smell density component.\\n   - 5-10 per KLOC: WARNING for the error handling smell density component.\\n   - Over 10 per KLOC: FAIL for the error handling smell density component.\\n   - Standalone test files are excluded from the smell count by the pre-pass; confirm that exclusion and do not double-count test files.\\n   - Examine each flagged smell location and report file:line references with specific remediation guidance. For example: Replace `.unwrap()` at store.rs:42 with `.context(\\\"failed to open connection pool\\\")?` or `.expect(\\\"pool initialized in main\\\")`.\\n\\n   - Backwards-compatibility code: old code paths kept \\\"just in case\\\" (renamed `_` variables, re-exports of removed types, `// removed` comments, deprecated shims). If unused, it should be deleted.\\n   - Fallback lookups: code that tries one path and falls back to another. There should be exactly one lookup path. If it fails, throw an error.\\n   - Belt-and-suspenders: redundant validation that duplicates what the framework, DB, or upstream already guarantees (re-checking auth after middleware, null-checking non-null TypeScript values, re-validating input that a validator already checked).\\n   - Feature flags for dead code: if the old way is being replaced, delete it. Don't gate behind a flag.\\n   - Defensive handling of impossible states: catch blocks or null checks for conditions that cannot occur given current code paths.\\n\\n8. **Code simplify:** Run /simplify against the changed files. \\n\\n9. **Security review:** If the diff touches auth, secrets, user input, network boundaries, or filesystem trust boundaries (copying/moving/promoting files between directories, extracting archives, sandbox-to-production transfers), run /security. Otherwise \\\"N/A.\\\"\\n\\n10. **Contract assertions:** Mark each assertion PASS, FAIL, or SKIP with a one-line explanation. SKIPs for work deferred to later tasks: append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <assertion>`.\\n\\n11. **Behavioral conformance:** For each assertion that describes observable behavior (CLI output, exit codes, file content, API response, shell behavior), write and execute a minimal test script that verifies the behavior by actually running the code. Do not rely solely on reading the code -- run it. Examples:\\n    - CLI flag assertion: run the binary with the flag, check output\\n    - Exit code assertion: run the command, check `$?`\\n    - Shell alias assertion: source the alias, run `type <name>`, check the definition\\n    - API response assertion: curl the endpoint, check the response body\\n    \\n    If an assertion cannot be tested via execution (e.g., internal architecture, code organization, build configuration), mark it as \\\"verified by inspection\\\" with an explanation of why execution was not possible.\\n    \\n    If a behavioral test is inconclusive (e.g., the test script errors out, the environment lacks a dependency), mark it as \\\"verified by inspection (execution inconclusive: <reason>)\\\" and fall back to code reading. Do not let an inconclusive execution check block the validation.\\n    \\n    Report each assertion's verification method (execution or inspection) in the contract assertions table.\\n\\n12. **Silent skip detection:** Grep test files in the diff for language-specific patterns that cause tests to report PASS while testing nothing. Use the `Detected language` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass to choose the primary language-specific scan, and also inspect file extensions in the diff so mixed-language projects are covered.\\n\\n    Test file detection uses the same language-specific patterns as the file size count exclusions: Rust files in `tests/` or files containing `#[test]`; TypeScript/Node `*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`, or files in `__tests__/`; Python `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, or files in `tests/`.\\n\\n    If the diff contains no test files, report `silent-skip-detection | SKIP | no test files in diff`.\\n\\n    Flag these Rust silent skip patterns:\\n    - Flag `else { return; }` or `else { return }` inside functions annotated with `#[test]` or `#[tokio::test]`.\\n    - Flag `if env::var(\\\"X\\\").is_err() { return; }` or similar top-of-test conditional returns based on environment variables.\\n    - Do not flag Rust test functions marked with `#[ignore]`.\\n\\n    Flag these TypeScript/Node silent skip patterns:\\n    - Flag `if (!process.env.X) return` inside `it()`, `test()`, or `describe()` blocks.\\n    - Flag `if (!process.env.X) { return; }` inside `it()`, `test()`, or `describe()` blocks.\\n    - Do not flag `it.skip()`, `describe.skip()`, or `@jest.skip`.\\n\\n    Flag these Python silent skip patterns:\\n    - Flag `if not os.environ.get(\\\"X\\\"): return` inside `test_` functions.\\n    - Flag bare `return` at the top of `test_` functions when it is conditional on environment variables.\\n    - Do not flag `@pytest.mark.skip` or `pytest.skip(\\\"reason\\\")` with an explicit message.\\n\\n    Any silent skip found is a FAIL because silent skips give false confidence by hiding missing coverage.\\n\\n    Report failures as `silent-skip-detection | FAIL | N silent skips found`. For each instance, list the file path, line number, test function name, and language-appropriate remediation guidance: Rust: \\\"fail explicitly with `panic!(\\\"requires X\\\")` or mark with `#[ignore]`\\\"; TypeScript: \\\"throw an error or use `it.skip()`\\\"; Python: \\\"call `pytest.skip(\\\"reason\\\")` or use `@pytest.mark.skip`\\\".\\n\\n13. **Diff coverage:** Use the `Detected language` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass to select the coverage tool. Run coverage for the primary language only. In multi-language projects, use the language with the most production source files in the diff; do not run two coverage tools.\\n\\n    First identify changed production source files for the selected language. Use the same production source and standalone test exclusions as the file size count:\\n    - Rust: include `.rs`; exclude files in `tests/`.\\n    - TypeScript/Node: include `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`, `.jsx`; exclude `*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`, and files in `__tests__/`.\\n    - Python: include `.py`; exclude `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, and files in `tests/`.\\n\\n    If the diff contains no production source files, report `diff-coverage | SKIP | no production source files in diff`.\\n\\n    Run the language-appropriate coverage tool:\\n    - Rust: run `cargo tarpaulin --out json` first; if that is unavailable or fails because the tool is missing, use `cargo llvm-cov --json` as fallback.\\n    - TypeScript/Node: run `npx jest --coverage --json` or `npx c8 --reporter json`.\\n    - Python: run `pytest --cov --cov-report json`.\\n\\n    If the coverage tool is not installed, first report `diff-coverage | SKIP | <tool> not installed` as a WARNING, then attempt auto-install as a fallback. For Rust, attempt `cargo install cargo-tarpaulin` before retrying tarpaulin. For TypeScript/Node and Python, use the project's existing package manager or environment only when that is clearly configured by the repository; otherwise keep the SKIP result and explain the missing tool. If auto-install succeeds, retry the coverage run. If auto-install fails, keep the SKIP result.\\n\\n    Run `git diff <commit-sha>^ <commit-sha> --name-only` to get changed files. Filter the coverage JSON to those changed files, then count only added lines from `git diff <commit-sha>^ <commit-sha>`. Added lines are lines that appear as additions in the task diff. This is diff coverage: line coverage for new code only, not whole-file or whole-project coverage.\\n\\n    Apply these thresholds to added-line coverage:\\n    - 80% or higher: PASS.\\n    - 70-79%: WARN, include it in the report, and do not fail the task.\\n    - Below 70%: FAIL and trigger the fix loop.\\n\\n    Report success as `diff-coverage | PASS | 85% coverage on changed lines`. Report warnings as `diff-coverage | WARN | 74% coverage on changed lines (target: 80%)`. Report failures as `diff-coverage | FAIL | 55% coverage on changed lines (minimum: 70%)`. Include details listing under-covered files with added-line ranges that lack coverage.\\n\\n14. **Test quality review:** If the diff contains no test files, report `test-quality | SKIP | no test files in diff`.\\n\\n    Run /test-quality as a subagent against the full diff, following the same delegation pattern as /review, /simplify, and /security. Read the skill markdown findings after the subagent completes. Findings are grouped by severity (P0, P1, P2, P3) and include file:line, evidence, and remediation.\\n\\n    Apply this severity-based result behavior:\\n    - Any P0 finding, including tautological assertions, is a FAIL.\\n    - 3 or more P1 findings are a FAIL. P1 findings include untested critical branches and behavioral changes without tests.\\n    - 1-2 P1 findings are WARNINGS; include them in the report but do not fail the task.\\n    - P2 and P3 findings only are WARNINGS; include them in the report but do not fail the task.\\n    - No findings is PASS.\\n\\n    Report passing output as `test-quality | PASS | no findings`. Report warning output as `test-quality | WARN | 2 P2, 1 P3 findings`. Report failing output as `test-quality | FAIL | 1 P0 finding (tautological assertion in test_list_open)`. Include a summary of findings in the notes column.\\n\\n## Output Format\\n\\nWrite your report to: <absolute path to mission/validations/task-N-scrutiny.md>\\n\\nUse this format:\\n\\n```markdown\\n# Scrutiny Validation: Task N\\n\\n## Result: PASS | FAIL\\n\\n## Gate Results\\n| Gate | Result | Notes |\\n|-------|--------|-------|\\n| Gate 1: Test presence | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 2: Build | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 3: Tests | PASS/FAIL | `<passed> passed, <failed> failed, <skipped_or_ignored> skipped/ignored (<pass_rate>% pass rate)`; include failure groups when failing and WARNING when actual run rate is below 90% |\\n| Gate 4: Lint | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 5: Code review | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 6: Module depth | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 7: Code standards | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 8: Code simplify | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 9: Security review | PASS/FAIL/N/A | ... |\\n| Gate 10: Contract assertions | PASS/FAIL | ... |\\n| Gate 11: Behavioral conformance | PASS/FAIL/N/A | ... |\\n| Gate 12: Silent skip detection | PASS/FAIL/SKIP | ... |\\n| Gate 13: Diff coverage | PASS/WARN/FAIL/SKIP | ... |\\n| Gate 14: Test quality review | PASS/WARN/FAIL/SKIP | ... |\\n\\n## Contract Assertions\\n| # | Assertion | Result | Method | Evidence |\\n|---|-----------|--------|--------|----------|\\n| 1 | <assertion text> | PASS/FAIL | execution/inspection | <one-line evidence> |\\n| 2 | ... | ... | ... | ... |\\n\\n## Failed Assertions (if any)\\n<detailed explanation of each failure>\\n\\n## Recommendations (if FAIL)\\n<specific guidance for fixing the failures>\\n```\\n\\nIMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY gate fails or ANY contract assertion fails. Be strict. A near-miss is still a FAIL.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/templates/contract.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/templates/contract.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/templates/contract.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Contract\\n\\n## Task 1: <name>\\n<!-- Specs: capability/1, capability/3 -->\\n- [ ] [execution] <assertion capturing specific spec scenario detail>\\n- [ ] [output] <assertion capturing specific spec scenario detail>\\n\\n## Task 2: <name>\\n<!-- Specs: capability/2, capability/4 (DEVIATED) -->\\n- [ ] [execution] <assertion capturing specific spec scenario detail>\\n- [ ] [inspection] <assertion capturing the deviated behavior as actually implemented>\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:08Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,90p' skills/mission/templates/plan.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,90p' skills/mission/templates/plan.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/templates/pr-body.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/templates/pr-body.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,90p' skills/mission/templates/plan.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Mission: <change-name>\\n\\n## Status: PLANNING | APPROVED | BUILDING | COMPLETE | PAUSED\\n\\n## State Directory\\nopenspec/changes/<change-name>/mission/\\n\\n## Darkfactory SHA\\n<full SHA of ~/.darkfactory at plan-creation time; append \\\"-dirty\\\" if working tree has uncommitted changes>\\nCaptured so this mission can be reproduced against the exact skill version that ran it.\\n\\n## OpenSpec Artifacts\\n- Proposal: openspec/changes/<change-name>/proposal.md\\n- Specs: openspec/changes/<change-name>/specs/<capability>/spec.md (one per capability)\\n- Design: openspec/changes/<change-name>/design.md\\n- Tasks: openspec/changes/<change-name>/tasks.md (includes mandatory closing tasks)\\n- Coverage Audit: openspec/changes/<change-name>/coverage-audit.md\\n- Contract: openspec/changes/<change-name>/contract.md\\n\\n## Spec Coverage Audit\\n- PRD issue: <GitHub issue URL from Phase 3>\\n- PRD issue number: <N>\\n- Coverage table: <pending | complete>\\n- Gaps found: <count or \\\"none\\\">\\n- Gaps resolved: <added N tasks | deferred N | skipped N>\\n\\n## Engine Config\\nDefaults defined in Phase 6 config.yaml. \\n\\n## Deterministic Anchors\\nFilled during Phase 4.3 (5 anchors) and Phase 4.3a (task strategy anchor). The orchestrator references these when populating worker briefs and making phase decisions.\\n\\n- Workspace layout: <e.g., \\\"two-crate workspace: core lib + cli binary\\\" (see design.md Architecture Decision)>\\n- Task decomposition strategy: <e.g., \\\"layered, 7 feature tasks + 5 closing\\\" (see Phase 4.3a approval)>\\n- Primary domain types: <e.g., \\\"Todo, TodoStatus, IronTodoError\\\" (see design.md Type Catalog)>\\n- Error handling strategy: <e.g., \\\"thiserror in core, never anyhow\\\" (see design.md Error Strategy)>\\n- Test strategy: <e.g., \\\"unit + integration, serial with DB\\\" (see design.md)>\\n- Chosen architecture: <e.g., \\\"workspace-with-lib-and-bin, scored 10/12\\\" (see design.md Architecture Decision)>\\n\\n## Skills to Use\\n- /grill -- alignment interview (planning)\\n- /language -- shared language (planning)\\n- /prd -- structured PRD from grill conversation (planning)\\n- openspec CLI -- create artifacts sequentially: proposal, specs, design, tasks (planning)\\n- /review -- code review (scrutiny validation)\\n- /simplify -- simplify pass (scrutiny validation)\\n- /security -- security check (scrutiny validation, sensitive tasks only)\\n- /tdd -- test-driven development for workers (build phase)\\n- /test-app -- browser tests (user-test validation)\\n- /demo-video -- frontend demo recording (post-validation)\\n- gh pr create --draft (completion, no skill needed)\\n- /opsx:archive -- archive change (post-merge)\\n\\n## Mission Flow\\nFor each unchecked task in tasks.md (skip `- [~]` tasks):\\n1. Read mission state from progress.md\\n2. Write brief to mission/briefs/task-N.md\\n3. Dispatch worker (engine from config.yaml)\\n4. Read handoff from mission/handoffs/task-N.md\\n5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\n6. If fail -> write to issues/, spawn fix worker, re-validate. Circuit breaker at 3.\\n7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\n8. If fail -> same fix/re-validate loop\\n9. If frontend task -> spawn demo video\\n10. Mark task done in tasks.md, update progress.md\\n\\nAfter all tasks complete:\\n11. Run final gates (build, test, lint, format)\\n12. Run spec delta check against coverage audit\\n13. Create draft PR\\n14. Run dual adversarial review (Codex + Claude): each walks mission commits one at a time, groups findings by task, then does a cross-task pass\\n15. Present both reports to human alongside PR link\\n\\n## Rules\\n- One worker at a time (serial execution)\\n- Workers never see contract.md\\n- Validators never see proposal.md or design.md\\n- On any failure, re-run both per-task validators after fix (scrutiny, user-test)\\n- Write all decisions to progress.md\\n\\n## Codebase Deltas\\n<Accumulated after each task passes validation. Used to populate \\\"What Previous Tasks Built\\\" in subsequent briefs. Generated from git diff, never from handoff reports.>\\n\\n## Current Task: <N or \\\"none\\\">\\n## Current Step: <step name or \\\"none\\\">\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,80p' skills/mission/templates/pr-body.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Closes #<PRD issue number from plan>\\n\\n## Mission: <change-name>\\n\\n### Tasks\\n| # | Task | Commit | Status |\\n|---|------|--------|--------|\\n| 1 | <name> | <short SHA> | Done |\\n| 2 | <name> | <short SHA> | Done |\\n\\n### Validation Results\\n| Task | Scrutiny | User-Test | Demo | Fix Attempts |\\n|------|----------|-----------|------|-------------|\\n| 1 | PASS | PASS | N/A | 0 |\\n| 2 | PASS | PASS | RECORDED | 1 |\\n\\n### Issues Encountered\\n<list any issues from mission/issues/, or \\\"None\\\">\\n\\n### Mission Stats\\n- Total tasks: <N>\\n- Total assertions: <M>\\n- Total fix attempts: <sum>\\n- Circuit breakers triggered: <count>\\n- Duration: <start to end>\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,40p' skills/mission/templates/worker-fix-brief.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,40p' skills/mission/templates/worker-fix-brief.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,60p' skills/mission-resume/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,60p' skills/mission-resume/SKILL.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,40p' skills/mission/templates/worker-fix-brief.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Fix Brief: Task N - <name> (Attempt <attempt+1>)\\n\\n## What Failed\\n<describe the observed symptoms -- error messages, unexpected behavior, test failures. Do NOT copy assertion text from the contract. Describe what went wrong, not what the contract expected.>\\n\\n## Diagnostic Triage\\n<paste the triage subagent's output from mission/issues/task-N-v<attempt>-triage.md. If triage was skipped or failed, write \\\"Triage unavailable -- diagnose from the validator output below.\\\">\\n\\n## Validator Output\\n<relevant error messages, build/test output, review findings -- symptoms only>\\n\\n## Commit to Fix\\n<SHA of the commit that failed validation>\\n\\n## Procedures\\n- Use `/tdd` to fix the issues described above\\n- Do not refactor or change anything unrelated\\n- Commit as a single commit with message: \\\"[task-N] fix: <description of what was fixed>\\\"\\n- Write handoff report to openspec/changes/<change-name>/mission/handoffs/task-N-fix-v<attempt+1>.md\\n\\n## Handoff Report Format\\n- Task: Task N - <name> (Fix attempt <attempt+1>)\\n- Commit SHA: <the full SHA>\\n- Approach: <what you fixed and why>\\n- Commands run: <commands and exit codes>\\n- Concerns: <anything uncertain>\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,60p' skills/mission-resume/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: mission-resume\\ndescription: Resume an interrupted Darkfactory mission from file state. Finds active missions, reconstructs the orchestrator plan from disk artifacts, detects orphaned commits, and continues the build loop from the next unchecked task.\\n---\\n\\n# Mission Resume\\n\\nResume a Darkfactory mission that was interrupted (laptop sleep, terminal close, crash, context compaction). All mission state lives on disk. This skill reads it, rebuilds the orchestrator plan, and picks up where the previous session left off.\\n\\n## Phase 1: Find Active Missions\\n\\nSearch for OpenSpec changes that have an active mission (unchecked tasks remaining).\\n\\n1. Glob for `openspec/changes/*/mission/config.yaml` to find all mission directories.\\n2. For each match, read the corresponding `openspec/changes/<change-name>/tasks.md`.\\n3. A mission is **active** if `tasks.md` contains at least one unchecked item (`- [ ]`). Tasks marked `- [~]` (skipped) are not considered unchecked.\\n4. Collect the list of active missions with their change name and progress count (checked vs total tasks).\\n\\n### Handle results\\n\\n- **No active missions found:** Report \\\"No active missions found.\\\" and stop.\\n- **Exactly one active mission:** Use it. Report \\\"Resuming mission: <change-name>\\\".\\n- **Multiple active missions:** List each one with its progress. Ask the user to pick one.\\n\\n## Phase 2: Read Mission State\\n\\nRead all artifacts for the selected mission:\\n\\n| File | Path | Purpose |\\n|------|------|---------|\\n| Tasks | `openspec/changes/<change-name>/tasks.md` | Which tasks are done, which remain |\\n| Progress log | `mission/progress.md` | Chronological record of what happened |\\n| Mission config | `mission/config.yaml` | Engine assignments |\\n| Contract | `contract.md` | Assertions grouped by task for validators |\\n| Proposal | `proposal.md` | What is being built and why |\\n| Design | `design.md` | Architecture decisions |\\n\\nAlso read existing handoff files in `mission/handoffs/` and validation reports in `mission/validations/` (including `task-N-scrutiny.md`, `task-N-usertest.md`). These reveal mid-cycle interruptions.\\n\\n## Phase 3: Detect Mid-Cycle State and Orphaned Commits\\n\\nCheck each unchecked task for partial completion. A task can be interrupted at various points. The resume skill detects where it stopped and resumes from that point.\\n\\nFor each unchecked task (skip `- [~]` skipped tasks):\\n\\n### 3a: Check for handoff with missing validations\\n\\nIf `mission/handoffs/task-N.md` exists (worker finished), check which validation reports exist:\\n\\n| Handoff | Scrutiny Report | User-test Report | Resume From |\\n|---------|----------------|-----------------|-------------|\\n| exists | missing | missing | Step 4 (scrutiny validation) |\\n| exists | exists (FAIL) | missing | Step 5a (fix loop with scrutiny failure) |\\n| exists | exists (PASS) | missing | Step 6 (user-test validation) |\\n| exists | exists (PASS) | exists (FAIL) | Step 5a (fix loop with user-test failure) |\\n| exists | exists (PASS) | exists (PASS) | Step 6a (demo video) then mark complete |\\n\\nLog to progress.md: `<timestamp> -- Task N: resuming from <step name>. Previous session interrupted mid-cycle.`\\n\\n### 3b: Detect orphaned commits\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:14Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:17Z\",\"elapsed_s\":30,\"last_activity\":\"tool ended: command_execution\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' docs/design-decisions.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' docs/design-decisions.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,60p' docs/reference_factory_talk.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,60p' docs/reference_factory_talk.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,110p' docs/provenance.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,110p' docs/provenance.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,60p' docs/reference_factory_talk.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: factory-missions-talk-reference\\ndescription: Reference material from Factory's \\\"Assembling Agent Teams\\\" talk at AI Engineer Europe -- core architecture details for the Missions multi-agent system\\ntype: reference\\noriginSessionId: d0be7010-3858-4b92-8153-32045af7ab9b\\n---\\nSource: Talk by Factory's core agent harness lead at AI Engineer Europe (April 2026). Slide images in `/Users/stuart/Downloads/talkimages/` (13 JPEGs). Full transcript and summary provided by Stuart in the initial conversation.\\n\\n## Key Architecture Details\\n\\n**Five multi-agent strategies (taxonomy):**\\n1. Delegation -- parent spawns sub-agents\\n2. Creator-Verifier -- separate agents for building vs checking\\n3. Direct Communication -- agents talk without coordinator (fragile)\\n4. Negotiation -- agents coordinate over shared resources\\n5. Broadcast -- one agent sends info to many\\n\\n**Missions combine 4 of 5** (delegation, creator-verifier, broadcast, negotiation) into a single workflow.\\n\\n**Three-role architecture:**\\n- **Orchestrators** -- plan features/milestones, ask clarifying questions, produce a \\\"validation contract\\\" (defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions)\\n- **Workers** -- implement one feature at a time with clean context, commit to git, structured handoff report (what was done, what's undone, commands/exit codes, issues, procedure compliance)\\n- **Validators** -- two types:\\n  - *Scrutiny Validator* -- test suite, type checking, lint, dedicated code review agents per feature\\n  - *User Testing Validator* -- QA engine that spawns the app, interacts via computer use, fills forms, clicks buttons, checks flows end-to-end\\n\\n**Execution model:**\\n- Serial feature execution (one worker at a time) to avoid conflicts\\n- Parallelization only for read-only ops (code search, API research) and within validators\\n- Correctness compounds over multi-day runs\\n\\n**Model strategy:**\\n- Model-agnostic: different LLMs for planning (slow reasoning), implementation (fast code fluency), validation (precise instruction following)\\n- Structure compensates for non-frontier models\\n- Almost all orchestration logic is in ~700 lines of prompts/skills, not hard-coded\\n\\n**Longest mission:** 16 days. Results: ~50% test coverage lines, ~90% code coverage. Most time spent on user-testing validation, not code generation.\\n\\n**Designed not to be obsolete:** when a better model drops, the system just gets smarter with no code changes needed.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,110p' docs/provenance.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Provenance\\n\\nHow Darkfactory relates to its two original sources: Factory.ai's multi-agent mission\\narchitecture and Matt Pocock's AI development skills. Reviewed against the current\\nimplementation (SKILL.md) as of 2026-04-12.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Factory.ai\\n\\nSource: [docs/reference_factory_talk.md](reference_factory_talk.md)\\n\\n### Faithfully implemented\\n\\n| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory implementation |\\n|---------|--------------------|----------------------------|\\n| Three-role architecture | Orchestrator, worker, validator as distinct roles | Orchestrator is the /mission session. Workers are dispatched subagents. Validators are separate subagents with isolated prompts. |\\n| Validation contract | \\\"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions\\\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but before the build loop. Workers never see it. |\\n| Adversarial separation | Workers don't see the contract, validators don't see the plan | Stricter than Factory: workers never see contract.md, validators never see proposal.md or design.md. |\\n| Serial execution | One worker at a time to avoid conflicts | Rule 1: \\\"One worker at a time. Never dispatch two workers in parallel.\\\" |\\n| Structured handoffs | Workers produce reports with what was done, commands, exit codes, issues | Handoff template: commit SHA, approach, commands run, concerns. |\\n| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build/test/lint/review/standards/simplify/security/assertions/behavioral conformance. User-test (Phase 8 Step 6): HTTP tests or browser. |\\n| Multi-model strategy | Different LLMs for different roles | config.yaml: workers default to Codex (GPT-5.4), validators to Claude, adversarial reviewers use one of each. |\\n| Read-only parallelization | Only parallelize read-only operations | Only parallel dispatch is the two adversarial reviewers in Phase 9, which are read-only. |\\n\\n### Implemented differently\\n\\n| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory difference |\\n|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\\n| User-test validator | \\\"Computer use\\\" QA engine that spawns the app and interacts | Uses HTTP tests and browser automation (/test-app). Same concept, less sophisticated execution. |\\n\\n### Not implemented\\n\\n| Concept | Factory description | Why not |\\n|---------|--------------------|---------| \\n| Negotiation pattern | Agents coordinating over shared resources | Darkfactory is purely orchestrator-mediated. No inter-agent coordination. Serial execution eliminates the need for resource negotiation. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Matt Pocock\\n\\nSource: [docs/reference_pocock_talk.md](reference_pocock_talk.md)\\n\\n### Faithfully implemented\\n\\n| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory implementation |\\n|---------|-------------------|-----------------------------|\\n| Adversarial alignment interview | 40-100 questions to reach shared understanding before any work | Phase 1: /grill. Adds a safeguard Pocock didn't describe: warns if user tries to skip. |\\n| Ubiquitous language | DDD-inspired glossary with canonical terms, aliases to avoid, relationships | Phase 2: /language. Glossary threaded through briefs, documentation, and all phases. |\\n| TDD as speed limit | Force small steps -- write test, make pass, refactor. Prevents AI from outracing its headlights. | Worker briefs mandate /tdd. Scrutiny validator enforces with automatic FAIL if diff has zero new tests (test presence gate). |\\n| Deep modules | Wrap related code in deep modules. Large functionality behind simple interfaces. Test at the interface. | Phase 4.3 (design) requires module depth analysis: describe the interface, what it hides, why that boundary. Flags shallow modules. |\\n\\n### Implemented differently\\n\\n| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory difference |\\n|---------|-------------------|-----------------------|\\n| Gray boxes | Treat deep modules as gray boxes. Design the interface, delegate internals to AI. Human selectively reviews internals for critical paths. | Automated via adversarial separation: validators review code without seeing design intent. Human reviews only the final adversarial reports and draft PR. More automated than Pocock's manual approach. |\\n| Strategic vs tactical | Include module changes and interface designs in PRDs. Human thinks strategically, AI programs tactically. | Replaced /prd-to-plan with a sequential artifact pipeline (proposal -> specs -> design -> tasks) with human gates at each step. Same goal, different mechanism. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## What Darkfactory adds\\n\\nConcepts in the current implementation that neither source described:\\n\\n| Addition | Purpose |\\n|----------|---------|\\n| Spec coverage audit with deviation classification | Maps every PRD requirement to tasks. Classifies deviations as interpretation, substitution, or removal with escalating approval. |\\n| Contract cross-referencing to PRD | Assertions trace to specific PRD requirements. Deviated items are marked. |\\n| Fix loop with circuit breaker | On validation failure, spawns a fix worker and re-runs both validators. Caps at 3 attempts before escalating to human. |\\n| Spec delta check | Post-build verification that every COVERED item in the coverage audit is actually present in the built code. |\\n| Final gates | Whole-repo build/test/lint/format after the last task catches regressions from late tasks. |\\n| Behavioral conformance | Scrutiny validator attempts to verify observable behavior by execution, not just code reading. |\\n| Dual adversarial review | Two independent engines (Codex + Claude) review the full diff in parallel at mission end. |\\n| File-backed state and resume | All decisions persisted to disk. Missions survive session death and can be resumed. |\\n| Mandatory closing tasks | Cleanup and documentation tasks after all feature tasks. |\\n| Demo video capture | Records frontend functionality after validation passes, for human review. |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' docs/design-decisions.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Darkfactory Design Decisions\\n\\nCaptured from the grill session on 2026-04-10.\\n\\n## Vision\\n\\nA lightweight multi-agent mission system built entirely from Claude Code primitives (skills, subagents, plan mode, hooks) and Codex CLI. No custom IDE or app. Inspired by Factory's Missions architecture.\\n\\n## Phase Flow\\n\\n```\\nalign -> spec -> contract -> build -> validate -> ship\\n```\\n\\nInner loop per task: `spec -> build -> verify -> ship` with repetition on validation failure.\\n\\n## Architecture\\n\\n### Orchestrator\\n- IS the main Claude Code session (Opus 4.6, not configurable -- it's the session)\\n- Enters plan mode at start -- the plan is the durable operating manual that survives compaction\\n- Interactive during planning (grills the user, proposes plan, gets approval)\\n- Autonomous during execution (runs the worker/validator loop)\\n- Manages all state via files on disk\\n- Spawns workers via Codex, validators via Claude subagents\\n\\n### Workers\\n- One worker per task (vertical slice pattern)\\n- Receive a brief file -- self-contained, includes task + proposal excerpt + design excerpt + procedures\\n- Do NOT receive the contract (adversarial separation)\\n- Do NOT receive previous workers' handoff reports (clean context)\\n- Commit all work in a single commit\\n- Write a structured handoff report to disk\\n- Default engine: Codex (configurable in mission/config.yaml)\\n\\n### Per-task validators (run sequentially)\\n1. **Scrutiny validator** -- test presence gate, build/test/lint, /review, code standards, /simplify, /security (conditional), contract assertions. Always runs.\\n2. **User-test validator** -- decides API tests (HTTP) and/or browser tests (agent-browser) based on what changed. Always runs.\\n3. **Demo video** -- recorded after both validators pass, for frontend tasks. Orchestrator decides when.\\n\\n### End-of-mission review\\n4. **Dual adversarial review** -- runs once after the draft PR is created, against the full diff. Codex and Claude independently review the code. Both reports are presented to the human. No automated fix loop -- the human decides what to address.\\n\\nValidators receive: commit SHA + contract assertions. They do NOT receive proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\\n\\n### Fix Loop\\n- On validation failure: orchestrator writes a symptom-based fix brief (no contract text) to mission/issues/, spawns a fix worker\\n- Fix worker uses `/tdd` to fix\\n- Fix worker commits a single fix commit\\n- Both per-task validators re-run after every fix (scrutiny + user-test)\\n- Circuit breaker: 3 attempts on the same task -> pause and ask human\\n- Skipped tasks marked with `- [~]` in tasks.md -- the build loop ignores them\\n\\n## Key Design Decisions\\n\\n### Bash run_in_background for external processes\\n- Long-running external processes (Codex workers, Codex adversarial reviews) run via Bash with `run_in_background: true`, with stdout+stderr redirected to a log file under `mission/logs/`\\n- Claude subagent validators use the Agent tool directly since they run within the session\\n- The log file lets the human watch progress in another terminal (`tail -f`) without the agent paying context cost for mid-run events\\n\\n### File-backed state\\n- All mission state lives on disk in the mission directory\\n- The orchestrator reads fresh state before each cycle (survives compaction)\\n- Enables session resume if Claude Code dies\\n- tmux as first line of defense against session death\\n\\n### Serial execution\\n- One worker at a time (matches Factory's approach)\\n- Parallelization only for read-only operations within validators\\n- Correctness compounds over time\\n\\n### Vertical slices\\n- Each task in tasks.md should be a thin vertical slice cutting through all layers\\n- A completed task is demoable and verifiable on its own\\n- One task = one worker = one commit = one validation cycle\\n\\n### Configurable engines\\n- mission/config.yaml specifies which engine per role (codex/claude)\\n- Default: Codex for workers and adversarial reviewer A, Claude for validators, adversarial reviewer B, and demo video\\n- Swap engines when new models drop without changing anything else\\n\\n### OpenSpec integration\\n- `/mission` is the single workflow for all work (small fixes to large epics). Scope adapts naturally.\\n- contract.md is a new OpenSpec artifact (validation assertions, written during planning)\\n- mission/ directory nests inside openspec/changes/<change-name>/\\n- Archiving the change archives the mission artifacts too\\n\\n### Entry point and completion\\n- `/mission` skill starts a mission\\n- `/mission-resume` resumes from file state\\n- `/mission-status` shows progress\\n- On completion: draft PR created via `gh pr create --draft`\\n- Human reviews and promotes to ready\\n\\n## Orchestrator Plan Structure\\n\\n```markdown\\n# Mission: [change-name]\\n\\n## State Directory\\nopenspec/changes/<change-name>/mission/\\n\\n## OpenSpec Artifacts\\n- Proposal: openspec/changes/<change-name>/proposal.md\\n- Design: openspec/changes/<change-name>/design.md\\n- Tasks: openspec/changes/<change-name>/tasks.md\\n- Contract: openspec/changes/<change-name>/contract.md\\n\\n## Engine Config\\n- Workers: codex\\n- Scrutiny validator: claude\\n- User-test validator: claude\\n- Adversarial reviewer A: codex\\n- Adversarial reviewer B: claude\\n- Demo video: claude\\n\\n## Skills to Use\\n- Alignment: /grill (during planning, before artifacts are created)\\n- Shared language: /language (during planning, establish terms)\\n- PRD: /prd (turn grill conversation into structured PRD)\\n- Planning: /opsx:propose (create proposal, design, tasks from PRD)\\n- Task breakdown: each task is a vertical slice through all layers, demoable and verifiable on its own\\n- Contract: written by orchestrator after planning, before workers start\\n- Worker implementation: /tdd (test-driven development for workers)\\n- Worker briefs: assembled from proposal.md + design.md + task description\\n- Scrutiny validation: build/test/lint + /review + /simplify against contract\\n- Security validation: /security (for tasks touching auth/sensitive paths)\\n- User-test validation: /test-app (agent-browser) and/or HTTP tests against API\\n- Adversarial review: dual Codex + Claude review in parallel, orchestrator synthesizes\\n- Demo video: /demo-video (for frontend tasks)\\n- Completion: gh pr create --draft (draft PR with mission summary)\\n- Archival: /opsx:archive (after merge)\\n\\n## Mission Flow\\nFor each unchecked task in tasks.md:\\n1. Read mission state from mission/progress.md\\n2. Write brief to mission/briefs/task-N.md\\n3. Kick off worker via Bash `run_in_background` (engine per config.yaml)\\n4. On completion, read mission/handoffs/task-N.md\\n5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\n6. If fail -> write symptom-based fix brief, spawn fix worker, re-validate both. Circuit breaker: 3 attempts -> ask human.\\n7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\n8. If fail -> same fix/re-validate loop\\n9. If frontend task -> spawn demo video\\n10. Mark task complete in tasks.md, update progress.md\\n\\nAfter all tasks complete:\\n11. Create draft PR\\n12. Run dual adversarial review (Codex + Claude) against full diff\\n13. Present both reports to human alongside PR link\\n\\n## Rules\\n- One worker at a time (serial execution)\\n- Workers never see contract.md\\n- Validators never see proposal.md or design.md\\n- On any failure, re-run BOTH validators after fix\\n- Write all decisions to progress.md\\n```\\n\\n## Mission Directory Structure\\n\\n```\\nopenspec/changes/<change-name>/\\n  .openspec.yaml\\n  proposal.md\\n  design.md\\n  tasks.md\\n  contract.md\\n  mission/\\n    config.yaml\\n    progress.md\\n    briefs/\\n      task-1.md\\n      task-2.md\\n    handoffs/\\n      task-1.md\\n      task-2.md\\n    validations/\\n      task-1-scrutiny.md\\n      task-1-usertest.md\\n      task-1-demo.mp4\\n    issues/\\n      task-1-v1.md\\n```\\n\\n## Worker Brief Format\\n\\n```markdown\\n# Worker Brief: Task N - [name]\\n\\n## Your Task\\n[Task description and acceptance criteria]\\n\\n## Project Context\\n[Relevant excerpt from proposal.md]\\n\\n## Architecture\\n[Relevant excerpt from design.md]\\n\\n## Procedures\\n- Implement the task as described\\n- Commit all changes in a single commit with message: \\\"[task-N] description\\\"\\n- Write your handoff report to mission/handoffs/task-N.md using the format below\\n\\n## Handoff Report Format\\n- Task: [which task]\\n- Commit SHA: [the SHA]\\n- Approach: [brief explanation of what you did and why]\\n- Commands run: [build/test/lint commands and exit codes]\\n- Concerns: [anything you're uncertain about]\\n```\\n\\n## Contract Format\\n\\n```markdown\\n# Contract\\n\\n## Task 1: [name]\\n- [Assertion verifiable from outside without knowing the code]\\n- [Another assertion]\\n\\n## Task 2: [name]\\n- [Assertion]\\n```\\n\\n## Fix Brief Format\\n\\n```markdown\\n# Fix Brief: Task N - [name] (Attempt 2)\\n\\n## What Failed\\n[Specific failed assertions from the validator report]\\n\\n## Validator Output\\n[Relevant error messages, test failures, review findings]\\n\\n## Commit to Fix\\n[SHA of the broken commit]\\n\\n## Procedures\\n- Fix only the issues described above\\n- Do not refactor or change anything unrelated\\n- Commit as a single commit with message: \\\"[task-N] fix: description\\\"\\n- Write handoff report to mission/handoffs/task-N-fix-v2.md\\n```\\n\\n## Build Order (Darkfactory itself)\\n\\n1. **Orchestrator skeleton** -- /mission skill, plan mode, grill, /opsx:propose, contract.md, mission directory\\n2. **Worker loop** -- brief writing, Codex invocation via Bash `run_in_background`, handoff reading\\n3. **Scrutiny validation** -- build/test/lint + /review + fix loop with circuit breaker\\n4. **User-test validation** -- agent-browser + HTTP tests\\n5. **Demo video + draft PR** -- completion phase\\n6. **Resume + status** -- /mission-resume, /mission-status skill\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '70,95p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '70,95p' README.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '70,95p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\nFrom that conversation, it writes a PRD (Product Requirements Document) -- a structured description of the problem, the solution, user stories, and testing approach. The PRD is filed as a GitHub issue and becomes the source of truth for everything that follows. At the end of the mission, the draft PR links back to this issue.\\n\\nNext, it creates a series of planning documents using [OpenSpec](https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec) (an open-source tool for structured change proposals). These documents are created one at a time, and you review each before the orchestrator moves on:\\n\\n- **Proposal** -- what you're building and why\\n- **Specs** -- detailed requirements written as WHEN/THEN rules (e.g. \\\"WHEN a user submits the login form with wrong credentials, THEN show an error message\\\")\\n- **Design** -- architecture decisions and technical approach. The design evaluates 2-3 architecture candidates against a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth) and picks the highest-scoring option. A mandatory gate verifies the design includes an architecture decision, module inventory, type catalog, error strategy, integration points, and test strategy; the Type Catalog names problem-space domain types and excludes technical helpers like CLI parsers, test fixtures, and generic containers.\\n- **Tasks** -- an ordered checklist of implementation work. Before generating tasks, the orchestrator presents a decomposition strategy (layered, vertical, or hybrid) with a target task count for your approval. Each task includes topology metadata -- which prior tasks it depends on, what it consumes, and what it produces.\\n\\nEvery tasks.md ends with a set of **mandatory closing tasks** that run after the feature work: a smoke test (one end-to-end test through the primary user flow against real dependencies), a cleanup task (remove dead code, unused deps, and leftover scaffolding), a module shape re-check (catch drift between design.md and what the code actually exposes, including Type Catalog domain type names -- required once a mission has 5+ feature tasks), a documentation task, and a CLAUDE.md + ARCH.md generation task (creates project convention files if they don't already exist). A CI configuration task is optional and added when relevant.\\n\\nAfter the tasks are defined, the orchestrator runs a **coverage audit**. This maps every requirement from the PRD to one or more tasks. If anything is missing, it flags it. If a requirement was intentionally changed or dropped, you approve that decision explicitly. For substitutions, the orchestrator also checks that the replacement approach is fully covered by existing tasks -- if the substitution drops something important, it's flagged as a gap. Nothing falls through the cracks by accident.\\n\\nContract assertions trace back to specific spec capability and scenario identifiers for end-to-end traceability.\\n\\nFinally, it writes a **contract** -- a checklist of things the finished code should do, written so they can be tested from the outside (by running the code, not by reading the source). For example: \\\"the login endpoint returns a 401 when given wrong credentials.\\\" Each item traces back to a specific spec scenario and is tagged with how it should be verified (by executing the code, checking its output, or inspecting the source). Before moving on, the orchestrator runs a testability check to catch any vague or untestable items early. You review the contract, confirm which AI models handle each role (see [Configuration](#configuration)), and approve launch.\\n\\n### Stage 2: The orchestrator builds and validates autonomously\\n\\nFor each task, the orchestrator sends a worker (Codex or Claude, your choice) a brief describing what to build. The worker writes the code, commits its changes, and writes a handoff report. Workers never see the contract -- this is intentional, and explained in [information walls](#information-walls) below.\\n\\nAfter the first task passes validation, the orchestrator runs a foundation audit -- checking that the committed code's module structure and type names match the design. This catches naming drift before it cascades into subsequent tasks.\\n\\nAfter each worker finishes, two validators check the work:\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '95,150p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:24Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '95,150p' README.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '150,240p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:24Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '150,240p' README.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '150,240p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\n### File-backed state\\n\\nAll mission state lives in files on disk, not in memory. This has two practical benefits: if your Claude Code session crashes or you close the terminal, you can resume the mission from where it left off. And you can open a second terminal to check progress while a mission is running.\\n\\n```\\nopenspec/changes/<change-name>/\\n  proposal.md              # What and why\\n  specs/<capability>/spec.md  # Formal WHEN/THEN requirements (one per capability)\\n  design.md                # How (architecture decisions)\\n  tasks.md                 # Task checklist (includes mandatory closing tasks)\\n  coverage-audit.md        # PRD requirement-to-task mapping\\n  contract.md              # What the code should do (traced to PRD, tagged by verification method)\\n  mission/\\n    config.yaml            # Engine settings per role\\n    progress.md            # Orchestrator's running log\\n    briefs/task-N.md       # Worker briefs\\n    handoffs/task-N.md     # Worker handoff reports\\n    validations/           # Validator reports, demo videos\\n    issues/                # Failure details per attempt\\n    logs/                  # Streaming output from workers and reviewers (tail -f to watch live)\\n```\\n\\n## Skills\\n\\nDarkfactory is controlled through skills -- reusable prompt templates you invoke with a slash command in Claude Code.\\n\\n| Skill | Purpose |\\n|-------|---------|\\n| `/mission` | Start an autonomous mission from scratch. Walks you through planning, dispatches workers, validates, and delivers a draft PR. |\\n| `/mission-resume` | Resume a mission that was interrupted (session crash, closed terminal). Detects exactly where it left off and continues through to the draft PR and code review. |\\n| `/mission-status` | Read-only progress check. Open a second terminal while a mission runs to see which tasks are done, in progress, or queued. |\\n| `/mission-init` | Check your project setup. Verifies all 12 skill symlinks, CLI tools, and coverage tools. Runs `openspec init` if needed. |\\n| `/grill` | Alignment interview: asks pointed questions until the goals, constraints, and edge cases are clear. Used in the mission planning phase. |\\n| `/prd` | Turns an interview into a structured PRD filed as a GitHub issue. Used in the mission planning phase. |\\n| `/language` | Creates a shared glossary (ubiquitous language) so you, the orchestrator, and all workers use the same terms. Used in the mission planning phase. |\\n| `/tdd` | Test-driven development guidance. Workers invoke this during the build phase. Companion files cover deep modules, interfaces, mocking, refactoring, and test patterns. |\\n| `/test-quality` | LLM-based semantic review of test effectiveness. The scrutiny validator runs this as a subagent after every worker commit. |\\n| `/security` | Security review. The scrutiny validator runs this when the diff touches auth, secrets, user input, or network/filesystem boundaries. |\\n| `/adversarial` | Dual-agent code review: dispatches a Codex reviewer and a Claude reviewer with identical input, then surfaces disagreements. |\\n| `/dispatch` | Run a coding task via `darkfactory-dispatch`. Accepts a task description inline or prompts for one. Presents structured outcome. |\\n\\n### Skill structure\\n\\n`/mission` is split across files so only the control flow is always in context:\\n\\n- `SKILL.md` -- control flow, rules, phase gates (auto-loaded)\\n- `templates/` -- artifacts the orchestrator fills in: `plan.md`, `coverage-audit.md`, `contract.md`, `worker-brief.md`, `worker-fix-brief.md`, `pr-body.md`, `design-template.md`, `claude-md.md`\\n- `prompts/` -- prompts handed to subagents: `scrutiny-validator.md`, `usertest-validator.md`, `spec-delta-checker.md`, `adversarial-reviewer.md`\\n\\nCompanion files are read on demand when the skill reaches the step that needs them.\\n\\n### Other skills used by `/mission`\\n\\nAll Darkfactory skills are bundled in this repo. `/mission` also calls two built-in Claude Code skills and accepts two optional project-specific skills:\\n\\n| Skill | Source | When it's used |\\n|-------|--------|---------|\\n| `/review` | Built-in Claude Code (required) | Scrutiny validator: code review step |\\n| `/simplify` | Built-in Claude Code (required) | Scrutiny validator: complexity check step |\\n| `/test-app` | Your project's Claude skills (optional) | User-test validator: browser-based testing |\\n| `/demo-video` | Your project's Claude skills (optional) | Records a demo of frontend changes after validation passes |\\n| `openspec` CLI | Installed by `npm install -g @fission-ai/openspec` | Planning phase: creates the structured artifacts (proposal, specs, design, tasks) |\\n| `/opsx:archive` | Bundled with Darkfactory | Post-merge: moves the change's planning files into the OpenSpec archive directory |\\n\\n`/review` and `/simplify` are mandatory -- the scrutiny validator always runs them. `/test-app` and `/demo-video` are optional and project-specific -- you write these yourself for your project if you want browser testing or demo recordings.\\n\\n### Configuration\\n\\nDarkfactory reads global defaults from `~/.darkfactory/config.yaml`:\\n\\n```yaml\\ndefault_model: gpt-5.5\\n```\\n\\nThe `/dispatch` and `/adversarial` skills use `default_model` for Codex dispatches. If the file or key is missing, they fall back to `gpt-5.5`.\\n\\nEach mission creates a `config.yaml` that controls which AI engine handles each role. You can change these before approving the plan:\\n\\n```yaml\\nengines:\\n  worker: codex                    # or claude\\n  scrutiny_validator: claude\\n  usertest_validator: claude\\n  adversarial_reviewer_a: codex\\n  adversarial_reviewer_b: claude\\n  demo_video: claude\\n```\\n\\nThe default uses Codex for workers and one of the two independent code reviewers, and Claude for everything else. You can set any role to either engine.\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:24Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '95,150p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\n1. **Scrutiny validator** -- a thorough quality check that runs a sequence of checks:\\n   - Do tests exist? (auto-fails the task if not)\\n   - Does the project build, do the tests pass, does the linter pass?\\n   - Code review and code standards check\\n   - Module depth review -- did the change make modules shallower or leak internal details?\\n   - Complexity check -- is the code more complex than it needs to be?\\n   - Security review (only when the changes touch auth, secrets, user input, network boundaries, or filesystem trust boundaries)\\n   - Contract check -- does the code do what the contract says it should?\\n   - Execution check -- actually runs the code to confirm it works, rather than just reading the source\\n   - Test health checks -- silent skip detection, diff coverage on changed lines, and test quality review\\n\\n2. **User-test validator** -- tests the feature the way a user would. It decides whether to hit an API with HTTP requests, drive a browser, or both, based on what the task changed.\\n\\nIf either validator finds problems, the orchestrator sends a fix worker a description of what went wrong. It only describes the symptoms -- it never reveals the contract, so the fix worker has to solve the real problem, not just game the checklist. After the fix, both validators re-run from scratch.\\n\\nThis loop repeats until the task passes or hits the **circuit breaker**. After 3 total attempts (1 initial build + 2 fix rounds), the orchestrator pauses and asks you: retry, skip this task, or abort the mission.\\n\\n### Stage 3: You review and merge\\n\\nAfter all tasks are complete, the orchestrator runs three final checks:\\n\\n1. **Full-repo build check** -- builds, tests, lints, and formats the entire repository (not just the changed files). This catches cases where a late task accidentally broke something an earlier task built.\\n2. **Requirements check** -- compares the built code against the coverage audit. Did you actually build everything you said you would? If something is missing, you decide whether to add a fix task, reclassify it as intentionally skipped, or accept the gap.\\n3. **Independent code review** -- two separate reviewers (Codex and Claude, running at the same time) each review all the changes against the full contract. Their reports are presented to you alongside the draft PR link. This is not an automated gate -- you read both reports and decide what to address.\\n\\nYou review the findings, promote the PR from draft to ready, and merge.\\n\\n## Architecture\\n\\n![Darkfactory architecture diagram](docs/architecture.png)\\n\\n### Three roles\\n\\nThe system has three roles, each handled by a separate AI agent:\\n\\n**Orchestrator** -- the main Claude Code session you interact with. It plans the work, sends out one worker at a time, reads their results, runs validators, and decides what to do next. It uses Claude Code's \\\"plan mode,\\\" which keeps its instructions alive even when the conversation gets long enough that older messages are dropped to save space.\\n\\n**Workers** -- each worker builds a single task. By default, workers are powered by Codex (OpenAI's coding agent), but you can use Claude instead. Codex workers are dispatched through `darkfactory-dispatch`, a Rust process supervisor that provides process group isolation, two-phase timeout (SIGTERM then SIGKILL), heartbeat events every 15 seconds, and structured JSON results. Claude workers use Claude Code's built-in Agent tool. A worker gets a brief with everything it needs: what to build, how the project is structured, coding rules to follow, which exact Type Catalog domain type names to use, which files to look at, and a summary of what previous tasks already built (file paths, function names, and types -- facts only, never reasoning from other workers). It makes its changes in a single commit and writes a report describing what it did. Workers never see the contract -- they only know what the brief tells them.\\n\\n**Validators** -- after each worker finishes, validators check the work against the contract. Two validators run back-to-back for every task: the scrutiny validator (a thorough quality check) and the user-test validator (which tests the feature the way a real user would). Validators never see the proposal or design documents -- they only see the code and the contract.\\n\\n### Information walls\\n\\nWorkers never see the contract. Validators never see the design. Why?\\n\\nIf a validator could see the design intent, it might look at the code and think \\\"this looks like what the design described\\\" without actually checking whether the code works. By keeping workers and validators blind to each other's information, the system forces validation to be based on what the code *does*, not what someone *intended* it to do.\\n\\nThe same principle applies when fixing problems. When a validator finds something wrong, the orchestrator tells the fix worker what the symptoms are -- but never reveals the contract. The fix worker has to solve the real problem, not just make a checklist item pass.\\n\\n### Fix loop\\n\\nWhen either validator fails a task, a diagnostic triage agent first reads the failure report and the code changes to pinpoint the likely root cause (which file, which line, what went wrong). The orchestrator then sends a fix worker the symptoms and triage findings -- but never the contract, so the information wall is maintained. After the fix, both validators re-run from scratch. If it fails again, another fix round runs.\\n\\nThe **circuit breaker** stops this from going forever. After 3 total attempts (the first build plus 2 fix rounds), the orchestrator pauses and asks you: retry (the counter keeps going -- it doesn't reset), skip this task, or abort the mission.\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:24Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '240,330p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:24Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '240,330p' README.md\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '240,330p' README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\n## Usage\\n\\n### Start a mission\\n\\n```\\n/mission Build a user authentication system with email/password login, session management, and a profile page\\n```\\n\\nThe orchestrator walks you through the planning phase interactively -- interviewing you, creating documents, and asking for your approval at each step. Once you approve the plan and confirm launch, it runs the build loop autonomously. You don't need to do anything until it finishes or hits the circuit breaker.\\n\\n### Check progress\\n\\nFrom another terminal while a mission runs:\\n\\n```\\n/mission-status\\n```\\n\\nThis shows a progress bar, the status of each task (queued, in progress, done), validation results, and fix attempt counts.\\n\\n### Resume after interruption\\n\\nIf your session crashes or you close the terminal:\\n\\n```\\n/mission-resume\\n```\\n\\nThis is a separate skill, not a flag on `/mission`. It reads the files on disk, figures out exactly where the mission was interrupted -- including tasks where the code was committed but validation never ran -- and continues from that point. Once all tasks are done, it creates a draft PR and runs the independent code review.\\n\\n### When it's done\\n\\nThe orchestrator creates a draft PR and presents you with two independent code review reports. Review the findings, promote the PR from draft to ready, and merge. Then archive the change -- this moves the planning files (proposal, specs, design, contract, etc.) into the OpenSpec archive so they're preserved but out of the way:\\n\\n```\\n/opsx:archive\\n```\\n\\n## When to use a mission\\n\\nThe workflow scales naturally to match the size of the work. A small bug fix produces a short interview, one task, and full validation. A large feature produces a long interview, many tasks, and the same validation per task. The process is the same regardless of scope.\\n\\n| Scope | What happens |\\n|-------|-------------|\\n| Quick bug fix | Short interview, 1 task, full validation, draft PR |\\n| Small feature | Medium interview, 1-2 tasks, full validation, draft PR |\\n| Multi-task feature | Long interview, many tasks, full validation per task, draft PR |\\n| Large refactor | Long interview (40+ questions), many tasks, full validation, draft PR |\\n| Overnight build | Same as above, run in tmux |\\n\\n## How it was built\\n\\nThe first version was hand-written during a `/grill` session that produced the design decisions and shared vocabulary. The second version was built by Darkfactory itself -- Claude workers generated the skills from the specs, and a Codex code review caught issues that a fix worker addressed. Everything since then has been iterative improvement, and the system is still actively evolving.\\n\\n## Key references\\n\\n- [Architecture](ARCH.md) -- living architecture document covering the three-role agent model, information walls, build loop, and planning constraints\\n- [Factory Missions talk](docs/reference_factory_talk.md) -- the multi-agent architecture that inspired this project\\n- [Matt Pocock skills talk](docs/reference_pocock_talk.md) -- design alignment, ubiquitous language, and TDD patterns\\n- [Provenance](docs/provenance.md) -- how Darkfactory relates to both sources: what's faithful, what's different, and what's new\\n- [Design decisions](docs/design-decisions.md) -- every architectural choice with rationale\\n- [Ubiquitous language](UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md) -- shared terminology used throughout the codebase\\n\\n## License\\n\\nMIT\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:24Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:32Z\",\"elapsed_s\":45,\"last_activity\":\"tool ended: command_execution\",\"interval_s\":15}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"There is an existing dirty change in `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` and an untracked mission directory. I’ll avoid staging unrelated work and only commit the 12 requested files plus the Task 3 handoff I create.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'find openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:34Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'find openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort'\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'find openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/.openspec.yaml\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/contract.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/coverage-audit.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/design.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/briefs/task-1-fix-v2.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/briefs/task-1.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/briefs/task-2.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/briefs/task-3.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/config.yaml\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/handoffs/task-1-fix-v2.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/handoffs/task-1.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/handoffs/task-2.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/issues/task-1-v1.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/logs/task-1-codex.log\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/logs/task-1-fix-v2-codex.log\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/logs/task-2-codex.log\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/logs/task-3-codex.log\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/progress.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-1-scrutiny-v2.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-1-scrutiny.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-1-usertest.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-2-scrutiny.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-2-usertest-v2.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/task-2-usertest.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/proposal.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/specs/contract-check-rename/spec.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/specs/validator-gate-rename/spec.md\\nopenspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/tasks.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:34Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc pwd\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:34Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc pwd\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc pwd\",\"aggregated_output\":\"/Users/stuart/.darkfactory\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:34Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_23\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git ls-files openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:34Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git ls-files openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language'\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_23\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git ls-files openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:34Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_24\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ve finished the context read. The edits are straightforward text renames in contract sections, with no schema or behavior changes.\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_25\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md | sed -n '178,196p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md | sed -n '178,196p'\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_25\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md | sed -n '178,196p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   178\\t8. **Code simplify:** Run /simplify against the changed files. \\n   179\\t\\n   180\\t9. **Security review:** If the diff touches auth, secrets, user input, network boundaries, or filesystem trust boundaries (copying/moving/promoting files between directories, extracting archives, sandbox-to-production transfers), run /security. Otherwise \\\"N/A.\\\"\\n   181\\t\\n   182\\t10. **Contract assertions:** Mark each assertion PASS, FAIL, or SKIP with a one-line explanation. SKIPs for work deferred to later tasks: append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <assertion>`.\\n   183\\t\\n   184\\t11. **Behavioral conformance:** For each assertion that describes observable behavior (CLI output, exit codes, file content, API response, shell behavior), write and execute a minimal test script that verifies the behavior by actually running the code. Do not rely solely on reading the code -- run it. Examples:\\n   185\\t    - CLI flag assertion: run the binary with the flag, check output\\n   186\\t    - Exit code assertion: run the command, check `$?`\\n   187\\t    - Shell alias assertion: source the alias, run `type <name>`, check the definition\\n   188\\t    - API response assertion: curl the endpoint, check the response body\\n   189\\t    \\n   190\\t    If an assertion cannot be tested via execution (e.g., internal architecture, code organization, build configuration), mark it as \\\"verified by inspection\\\" with an explanation of why execution was not possible.\\n   191\\t    \\n   192\\t    If a behavioral test is inconclusive (e.g., the test script errors out, the environment lacks a dependency), mark it as \\\"verified by inspection (execution inconclusive: <reason>)\\\" and fall back to code reading. Do not let an inconclusive execution check block the validation.\\n   193\\t    \\n   194\\t    Report each assertion's verification method (execution or inspection) in the contract assertions table.\\n   195\\t\\n   196\\t12. **Silent skip detection:** Grep test files in the diff for language-specific patterns that cause tests to report PASS while testing nothing. Use the `Detected language` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass to choose the primary language-specific scan, and also inspect file extensions in the diff so mixed-language projects are covered.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_26\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design-decisions.md | sed -n '30,235p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design-decisions.md | sed -n '30,235p'\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_27\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/provenance.md | sed -n '14,75p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/provenance.md | sed -n '14,75p'\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md | sed -n '1,65p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"command_run\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md | sed -n '1,65p'\\\"\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_26\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design-decisions.md | sed -n '30,235p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    30\\t- Do NOT receive the contract (adversarial separation)\\n    31\\t- Do NOT receive previous workers' handoff reports (clean context)\\n    32\\t- Commit all work in a single commit\\n    33\\t- Write a structured handoff report to disk\\n    34\\t- Default engine: Codex (configurable in mission/config.yaml)\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\t### Per-task validators (run sequentially)\\n    37\\t1. **Scrutiny validator** -- test presence gate, build/test/lint, /review, code standards, /simplify, /security (conditional), contract assertions. Always runs.\\n    38\\t2. **User-test validator** -- decides API tests (HTTP) and/or browser tests (agent-browser) based on what changed. Always runs.\\n    39\\t3. **Demo video** -- recorded after both validators pass, for frontend tasks. Orchestrator decides when.\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\t### End-of-mission review\\n    42\\t4. **Dual adversarial review** -- runs once after the draft PR is created, against the full diff. Codex and Claude independently review the code. Both reports are presented to the human. No automated fix loop -- the human decides what to address.\\n    43\\t\\n    44\\tValidators receive: commit SHA + contract assertions. They do NOT receive proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\\n    45\\t\\n    46\\t### Fix Loop\\n    47\\t- On validation failure: orchestrator writes a symptom-based fix brief (no contract text) to mission/issues/, spawns a fix worker\\n    48\\t- Fix worker uses `/tdd` to fix\\n    49\\t- Fix worker commits a single fix commit\\n    50\\t- Both per-task validators re-run after every fix (scrutiny + user-test)\\n    51\\t- Circuit breaker: 3 attempts on the same task -> pause and ask human\\n    52\\t- Skipped tasks marked with `- [~]` in tasks.md -- the build loop ignores them\\n    53\\t\\n    54\\t## Key Design Decisions\\n    55\\t\\n    56\\t### Bash run_in_background for external processes\\n    57\\t- Long-running external processes (Codex workers, Codex adversarial reviews) run via Bash with `run_in_background: true`, with stdout+stderr redirected to a log file under `mission/logs/`\\n    58\\t- Claude subagent validators use the Agent tool directly since they run within the session\\n    59\\t- The log file lets the human watch progress in another terminal (`tail -f`) without the agent paying context cost for mid-run events\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t### File-backed state\\n    62\\t- All mission state lives on disk in the mission directory\\n    63\\t- The orchestrator reads fresh state before each cycle (survives compaction)\\n    64\\t- Enables session resume if Claude Code dies\\n    65\\t- tmux as first line of defense against session death\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\t### Serial execution\\n    68\\t- One worker at a time (matches Factory's approach)\\n    69\\t- Parallelization only for read-only operations within validators\\n    70\\t- Correctness compounds over time\\n    71\\t\\n    72\\t### Vertical slices\\n    73\\t- Each task in tasks.md should be a thin vertical slice cutting through all layers\\n    74\\t- A completed task is demoable and verifiable on its own\\n    75\\t- One task = one worker = one commit = one validation cycle\\n    76\\t\\n    77\\t### Configurable engines\\n    78\\t- mission/config.yaml specifies which engine per role (codex/claude)\\n    79\\t- Default: Codex for workers and adversarial reviewer A, Claude for validators, adversarial reviewer B, and demo video\\n    80\\t- Swap engines when new models drop without changing anything else\\n    81\\t\\n    82\\t### OpenSpec integration\\n    83\\t- `/mission` is the single workflow for all work (small fixes to large epics). Scope adapts naturally.\\n    84\\t- contract.md is a new OpenSpec artifact (validation assertions, written during planning)\\n    85\\t- mission/ directory nests inside openspec/changes/<change-name>/\\n    86\\t- Archiving the change archives the mission artifacts too\\n    87\\t\\n    88\\t### Entry point and completion\\n    89\\t- `/mission` skill starts a mission\\n    90\\t- `/mission-resume` resumes from file state\\n    91\\t- `/mission-status` shows progress\\n    92\\t- On completion: draft PR created via `gh pr create --draft`\\n    93\\t- Human reviews and promotes to ready\\n    94\\t\\n    95\\t## Orchestrator Plan Structure\\n    96\\t\\n    97\\t```markdown\\n    98\\t# Mission: [change-name]\\n    99\\t\\n   100\\t## State Directory\\n   101\\topenspec/changes/<change-name>/mission/\\n   102\\t\\n   103\\t## OpenSpec Artifacts\\n   104\\t- Proposal: openspec/changes/<change-name>/proposal.md\\n   105\\t- Design: openspec/changes/<change-name>/design.md\\n   106\\t- Tasks: openspec/changes/<change-name>/tasks.md\\n   107\\t- Contract: openspec/changes/<change-name>/contract.md\\n   108\\t\\n   109\\t## Engine Config\\n   110\\t- Workers: codex\\n   111\\t- Scrutiny validator: claude\\n   112\\t- User-test validator: claude\\n   113\\t- Adversarial reviewer A: codex\\n   114\\t- Adversarial reviewer B: claude\\n   115\\t- Demo video: claude\\n   116\\t\\n   117\\t## Skills to Use\\n   118\\t- Alignment: /grill (during planning, before artifacts are created)\\n   119\\t- Shared language: /language (during planning, establish terms)\\n   120\\t- PRD: /prd (turn grill conversation into structured PRD)\\n   121\\t- Planning: /opsx:propose (create proposal, design, tasks from PRD)\\n   122\\t- Task breakdown: each task is a vertical slice through all layers, demoable and verifiable on its own\\n   123\\t- Contract: written by orchestrator after planning, before workers start\\n   124\\t- Worker implementation: /tdd (test-driven development for workers)\\n   125\\t- Worker briefs: assembled from proposal.md + design.md + task description\\n   126\\t- Scrutiny validation: build/test/lint + /review + /simplify against contract\\n   127\\t- Security validation: /security (for tasks touching auth/sensitive paths)\\n   128\\t- User-test validation: /test-app (agent-browser) and/or HTTP tests against API\\n   129\\t- Adversarial review: dual Codex + Claude review in parallel, orchestrator synthesizes\\n   130\\t- Demo video: /demo-video (for frontend tasks)\\n   131\\t- Completion: gh pr create --draft (draft PR with mission summary)\\n   132\\t- Archival: /opsx:archive (after merge)\\n   133\\t\\n   134\\t## Mission Flow\\n   135\\tFor each unchecked task in tasks.md:\\n   136\\t1. Read mission state from mission/progress.md\\n   137\\t2. Write brief to mission/briefs/task-N.md\\n   138\\t3. Kick off worker via Bash `run_in_background` (engine per config.yaml)\\n   139\\t4. On completion, read mission/handoffs/task-N.md\\n   140\\t5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\n   141\\t6. If fail -> write symptom-based fix brief, spawn fix worker, re-validate both. Circuit breaker: 3 attempts -> ask human.\\n   142\\t7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\\n   143\\t8. If fail -> same fix/re-validate loop\\n   144\\t9. If frontend task -> spawn demo video\\n   145\\t10. Mark task complete in tasks.md, update progress.md\\n   146\\t\\n   147\\tAfter all tasks complete:\\n   148\\t11. Create draft PR\\n   149\\t12. Run dual adversarial review (Codex + Claude) against full diff\\n   150\\t13. Present both reports to human alongside PR link\\n   151\\t\\n   152\\t## Rules\\n   153\\t- One worker at a time (serial execution)\\n   154\\t- Workers never see contract.md\\n   155\\t- Validators never see proposal.md or design.md\\n   156\\t- On any failure, re-run BOTH validators after fix\\n   157\\t- Write all decisions to progress.md\\n   158\\t```\\n   159\\t\\n   160\\t## Mission Directory Structure\\n   161\\t\\n   162\\t```\\n   163\\topenspec/changes/<change-name>/\\n   164\\t  .openspec.yaml\\n   165\\t  proposal.md\\n   166\\t  design.md\\n   167\\t  tasks.md\\n   168\\t  contract.md\\n   169\\t  mission/\\n   170\\t    config.yaml\\n   171\\t    progress.md\\n   172\\t    briefs/\\n   173\\t      task-1.md\\n   174\\t      task-2.md\\n   175\\t    handoffs/\\n   176\\t      task-1.md\\n   177\\t      task-2.md\\n   178\\t    validations/\\n   179\\t      task-1-scrutiny.md\\n   180\\t      task-1-usertest.md\\n   181\\t      task-1-demo.mp4\\n   182\\t    issues/\\n   183\\t      task-1-v1.md\\n   184\\t```\\n   185\\t\\n   186\\t## Worker Brief Format\\n   187\\t\\n   188\\t```markdown\\n   189\\t# Worker Brief: Task N - [name]\\n   190\\t\\n   191\\t## Your Task\\n   192\\t[Task description and acceptance criteria]\\n   193\\t\\n   194\\t## Project Context\\n   195\\t[Relevant excerpt from proposal.md]\\n   196\\t\\n   197\\t## Architecture\\n   198\\t[Relevant excerpt from design.md]\\n   199\\t\\n   200\\t## Procedures\\n   201\\t- Implement the task as described\\n   202\\t- Commit all changes in a single commit with message: \\\"[task-N] description\\\"\\n   203\\t- Write your handoff report to mission/handoffs/task-N.md using the format below\\n   204\\t\\n   205\\t## Handoff Report Format\\n   206\\t- Task: [which task]\\n   207\\t- Commit SHA: [the SHA]\\n   208\\t- Approach: [brief explanation of what you did and why]\\n   209\\t- Commands run: [build/test/lint commands and exit codes]\\n   210\\t- Concerns: [anything you're uncertain about]\\n   211\\t```\\n   212\\t\\n   213\\t## Contract Format\\n   214\\t\\n   215\\t```markdown\\n   216\\t# Contract\\n   217\\t\\n   218\\t## Task 1: [name]\\n   219\\t- [Assertion verifiable from outside without knowing the code]\\n   220\\t- [Another assertion]\\n   221\\t\\n   222\\t## Task 2: [name]\\n   223\\t- [Assertion]\\n   224\\t```\\n   225\\t\\n   226\\t## Fix Brief Format\\n   227\\t\\n   228\\t```markdown\\n   229\\t# Fix Brief: Task N - [name] (Attempt 2)\\n   230\\t\\n   231\\t## What Failed\\n   232\\t[Specific failed assertions from the validator report]\\n   233\\t\\n   234\\t## Validator Output\\n   235\\t[Relevant error messages, test failures, review findings]\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_27\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/provenance.md | sed -n '14,75p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    14\\t\\n    15\\t| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory implementation |\\n    16\\t|---------|--------------------|----------------------------|\\n    17\\t| Three-role architecture | Orchestrator, worker, validator as distinct roles | Orchestrator is the /mission session. Workers are dispatched subagents. Validators are separate subagents with isolated prompts. |\\n    18\\t| Validation contract | \\\"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions\\\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but before the build loop. Workers never see it. |\\n    19\\t| Adversarial separation | Workers don't see the contract, validators don't see the plan | Stricter than Factory: workers never see contract.md, validators never see proposal.md or design.md. |\\n    20\\t| Serial execution | One worker at a time to avoid conflicts | Rule 1: \\\"One worker at a time. Never dispatch two workers in parallel.\\\" |\\n    21\\t| Structured handoffs | Workers produce reports with what was done, commands, exit codes, issues | Handoff template: commit SHA, approach, commands run, concerns. |\\n    22\\t| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build/test/lint/review/standards/simplify/security/assertions/behavioral conformance. User-test (Phase 8 Step 6): HTTP tests or browser. |\\n    23\\t| Multi-model strategy | Different LLMs for different roles | config.yaml: workers default to Codex (GPT-5.4), validators to Claude, adversarial reviewers use one of each. |\\n    24\\t| Read-only parallelization | Only parallelize read-only operations | Only parallel dispatch is the two adversarial reviewers in Phase 9, which are read-only. |\\n    25\\t\\n    26\\t### Implemented differently\\n    27\\t\\n    28\\t| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory difference |\\n    29\\t|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\\n    30\\t| User-test validator | \\\"Computer use\\\" QA engine that spawns the app and interacts | Uses HTTP tests and browser automation (/test-app). Same concept, less sophisticated execution. |\\n    31\\t\\n    32\\t### Not implemented\\n    33\\t\\n    34\\t| Concept | Factory description | Why not |\\n    35\\t|---------|--------------------|---------| \\n    36\\t| Negotiation pattern | Agents coordinating over shared resources | Darkfactory is purely orchestrator-mediated. No inter-agent coordination. Serial execution eliminates the need for resource negotiation. |\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\t---\\n    39\\t\\n    40\\t## Matt Pocock\\n    41\\t\\n    42\\tSource: [docs/reference_pocock_talk.md](reference_pocock_talk.md)\\n    43\\t\\n    44\\t### Faithfully implemented\\n    45\\t\\n    46\\t| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory implementation |\\n    47\\t|---------|-------------------|-----------------------------|\\n    48\\t| Adversarial alignment interview | 40-100 questions to reach shared understanding before any work | Phase 1: /grill. Adds a safeguard Pocock didn't describe: warns if user tries to skip. |\\n    49\\t| Ubiquitous language | DDD-inspired glossary with canonical terms, aliases to avoid, relationships | Phase 2: /language. Glossary threaded through briefs, documentation, and all phases. |\\n    50\\t| TDD as speed limit | Force small steps -- write test, make pass, refactor. Prevents AI from outracing its headlights. | Worker briefs mandate /tdd. Scrutiny validator enforces with automatic FAIL if diff has zero new tests (test presence gate). |\\n    51\\t| Deep modules | Wrap related code in deep modules. Large functionality behind simple interfaces. Test at the interface. | Phase 4.3 (design) requires module depth analysis: describe the interface, what it hides, why that boundary. Flags shallow modules. |\\n    52\\t\\n    53\\t### Implemented differently\\n    54\\t\\n    55\\t| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory difference |\\n    56\\t|---------|-------------------|-----------------------|\\n    57\\t| Gray boxes | Treat deep modules as gray boxes. Design the interface, delegate internals to AI. Human selectively reviews internals for critical paths. | Automated via adversarial separation: validators review code without seeing design intent. Human reviews only the final adversarial reports and draft PR. More automated than Pocock's manual approach. |\\n    58\\t| Strategic vs tactical | Include module changes and interface designs in PRDs. Human thinks strategically, AI programs tactically. | Replaced /prd-to-plan with a sequential artifact pipeline (proposal -> specs -> design -> tasks) with human gates at each step. Same goal, different mechanism. |\\n    59\\t\\n    60\\t---\\n    61\\t\\n    62\\t## What Darkfactory adds\\n    63\\t\\n    64\\tConcepts in the current implementation that neither source described:\\n    65\\t\\n    66\\t| Addition | Purpose |\\n    67\\t|----------|---------|\\n    68\\t| Spec coverage audit with deviation classification | Maps every PRD requirement to tasks. Classifies deviations as interpretation, substitution, or removal with escalating approval. |\\n    69\\t| Contract cross-referencing to PRD | Assertions trace to specific PRD requirements. Deviated items are marked. |\\n    70\\t| Fix loop with circuit breaker | On validation failure, spawns a fix worker and re-runs both validators. Caps at 3 attempts before escalating to human. |\\n    71\\t| Spec delta check | Post-build verification that every COVERED item in the coverage audit is actually present in the built code. |\\n    72\\t| Final gates | Whole-repo build/test/lint/format after the last task catches regressions from late tasks. |\\n    73\\t| Behavioral conformance | Scrutiny validator attempts to verify observable behavior by execution, not just code reading. |\\n    74\\t| Dual adversarial review | Two independent engines (Codex + Claude) review the full diff in parallel at mission end. |\\n    75\\t| File-backed state and resume | All decisions persisted to disk. Missions survive session death and can be resumed. |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md | sed -n '1,65p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\tYou are a user-test validator for the Darkfactory mission system. Your job is to verify that a worker's commit delivers the correct user-facing behavior. You have NOT seen the proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\t## Commit to Validate\\n     4\\tSHA: <commit-sha>\\n     5\\t\\n     6\\t## Contract Assertions for This Task\\n     7\\t<paste the assertions from contract.md for task N>\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\t## Instructions\\n    10\\t\\n    11\\t1. **Read the diff:** Run `git show <commit-sha>` to understand what changed.\\n    12\\t\\n    13\\t2. **Decide your test strategy** based on what the diff touches:\\n    14\\t   - **Backend only** (API routes, database, server logic, CLI): Write and run HTTP tests against the API endpoints. Use curl or a test script.\\n    15\\t   - **Frontend only** (UI components, pages, styles): Run /test-app to drive a browser and verify UI behavior.\\n    16\\t   - **Both backend and frontend**: Run HTTP tests first, then /test-app for the UI.\\n    17\\t   - **Infrastructure only** (config, build, CI): Verify the build passes and config is correct. You may mark behavioral assertions as SKIP with reason \\\"infrastructure-only change, not testable via HTTP or browser.\\\"\\n    18\\t\\n    19\\t3. **Check each contract assertion:** For each assertion, run the appropriate test. Mark PASS, FAIL, or SKIP.\\n    20\\t   - PASS: The assertion is demonstrably true.\\n    21\\t   - FAIL: The assertion is demonstrably false, or the test reveals a bug.\\n    22\\t   - SKIP: The assertion cannot be tested from outside (e.g., internal refactor) or the work is deferred to a later task. Provide a reason. If deferred to a later task, append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <assertion>`.\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\t## Output Format\\n    25\\t\\n    26\\tWrite your report to: <absolute path to mission/validations/task-N-usertest.md>\\n    27\\t\\n    28\\tUse this format:\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\t```markdown\\n    31\\t# User-Test Validation: Task N\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\t## Result: PASS | FAIL\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\t## Test Strategy\\n    36\\t<what you decided and why>\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\t## Tests Run\\n    39\\t| # | Test Description | Method | Result |\\n    40\\t|---|-----------------|--------|--------|\\n    41\\t| 1 | <what was tested> | HTTP/Browser/Manual | PASS/FAIL |\\n    42\\t| 2 | ... | ... | ... |\\n    43\\t\\n    44\\t## Contract Assertions\\n    45\\t| # | Assertion | Result | Evidence |\\n    46\\t|---|-----------|--------|----------|\\n    47\\t| 1 | <assertion text> | PASS/FAIL/SKIP | <evidence or skip reason> |\\n    48\\t| 2 | ... | ... | ... |\\n    49\\t\\n    50\\t## Failed Assertions (if any)\\n    51\\t<detailed explanation of each failure>\\n    52\\t\\n    53\\t## Recommendations (if FAIL)\\n    54\\t<specific guidance for fixing the failures>\\n    55\\t```\\n    56\\t\\n    57\\tIMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY contract assertion fails (SKIP does not count as failure). Be thorough -- actually run the tests, don't just read the code and guess.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_end\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:42Z\",\"tool\":\"command_execution\"}\n+{\"type\":\"heartbeat\",\"dispatch_id\":\"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-01T13:41:47Z\",\"elapsed_s\":60,\"last_a