{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git show 9d2e32a'","aggregated_output":"commit 9d2e32a7830f18d33fda97c1d8527c0f8b25ce1b\nAuthor: Stuart Bowness <1020179+stuartbowness@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Sun May 3 17:08:10 2026 -0700\n\n    [task-10] Update docs — README skills table, UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md gate refs, ARCH.md skill count\n\ndiff --git a/ARCH.md b/ARCH.md\nindex 56c41f7..8c9e6bd 100644\n--- a/ARCH.md\n+++ b/ARCH.md\n@@ -50,7 +50,7 @@ The mission skill is intentionally monolithic (~900 lines). Companion files are\n - `templates/` -- artifact formats (plan, contract, worker brief, design, etc.)\n - `prompts/` -- instructions handed to validator and reviewer subagents\n \n-12 skills total: mission, mission-resume, mission-status, mission-init, grill, language, prd, tdd, test-quality, security, adversarial, dispatch.\n+20 skills total: mission, mission-resume, mission-status, mission-init, grill, language, prd, tdd, test-quality, security, adversarial, dispatch, arch-review, domain-model, module-design, qa, write-a-skill, frontend, ui-system, react-perf.\n \n ## Build loop\n \ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex 617d5a1..3a011b9 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -183,7 +183,10 @@ Darkfactory is controlled through skills -- reusable prompt templates you invoke\n | `/grill` | Alignment interview: asks pointed questions until the goals, constraints, and edge cases are clear. Used in the mission planning phase. |\n | `/prd` | Turns an interview into a structured PRD filed as a GitHub issue. Used in the mission planning phase. |\n | `/language` | Creates a shared glossary (ubiquitous language) so you, the orchestrator, and all workers use the same terms. Used in the mission planning phase. |\n-| `/tdd` | Test-driven development guidance. Workers invoke this during the build phase. Companion files cover deep modules, interfaces, mocking, refactoring, and test patterns. |\n+| `/tdd` | Test-driven development guidance. Workers invoke this during the build phase for backend, hooks, and utility tasks. Companion files cover deep modules, interfaces, mocking, refactoring, and test patterns. |\n+| `/frontend` | Prescriptive builder guide for React/TypeScript component development. Workers invoke this during the build phase for frontend component, page, and layout tasks. Defines the design-implement-test workflow with visual verification via chrome-devtools-mcp. Companion files: DEEP-COMPONENTS.md (four rules), STACK.md (tech stack), PATTERNS.md (code examples), TESTING.md (behavioral testing). |\n+| `/ui-system` | ShadCN/Radix component selection, token discipline, composition patterns, and accessibility defaults. Constraint skill used alongside `/frontend`. Verbatim upstream content from shadcn/ui. |\n+| `/react-perf` | React and Next.js performance optimization from Vercel Engineering. Waterfall elimination, bundle size, re-render discipline, server-side patterns. Constraint skill used alongside `/frontend`. Verbatim upstream content from Vercel. |\n | `/test-quality` | LLM-based semantic review of test effectiveness. The scrutiny validator runs this as a subagent after every worker commit. |\n | `/security` | Security review. The scrutiny validator runs this when the diff touches auth, secrets, user input, or network/filesystem boundaries. |\n | `/adversarial` | Dual-agent code review: dispatches a Codex reviewer and a Claude reviewer with identical input, then surfaces disagreements. |\ndiff --git a/UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md b/UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\nindex 92ddbe5..e283316 100644\n--- a/UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\n+++ b/UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\n@@ -25,10 +25,10 @@\n \n | Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n | --- | --- | --- |\n-| **Gate** | A numbered step in the scrutiny validator's sequence (e.g., gate 3: tests, gate 10: contract checks). Gates report PASS, FAIL, WARN, SKIP, or N/A. | Check (in validator-step context), step, stage |\n+| **Gate** | A numbered step in the scrutiny validator's sequence (e.g., gate 3: tests, gate 11: contract checks). Gates report PASS, FAIL, WARN, SKIP, or N/A. | Check (in validator-step context), step, stage |\n | **Scrutiny validator** | The first validator type that runs build, test, lint, code review, code simplify, and test health gates against the contract | Code reviewer, linter, static validator |\n | **User-test validator** | The second validator type that tests behavior by hitting the API (HTTP tests) or driving a browser (agent-browser) | QA, E2E tester, integration tester |\n-| **Behavioral conformance** | A scrutiny validator gate (gate 11) that verifies observable behavior by writing and executing a test script rather than reading the code. Falls back to \"verified by inspection\" if execution is inconclusive. | Behavioral test, execution test, runtime check |\n+| **Behavioral conformance** | A scrutiny validator gate (gate 12) that verifies observable behavior by writing and executing a test script rather than reading the code. Falls back to \"verified by inspection\" if execution is inconclusive. | Behavioral test, execution test, runtime check |\n | **Final gates** | Whole-repo build, test, lint, and format gates run after the last task completes but before PR creation. Catches regressions introduced by later tasks (e.g., documentation or CI tasks) after the cleanup task ran. | Final checks, post-build gates, repo gates |\n | **Module shape re-check** | A mandatory closing task that compares design.md module descriptions and Type Catalog names to what the code actually exposes, catching cumulative drift across tasks that per-task scrutiny cannot see | Shape check, surface audit, drift check |\n | **Demo video** | A recording of frontend functionality captured after both validators pass, created for human review | Screencast, screen recording |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
