{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/validations/adversarial-a.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review A: frontend-architecture-skill\n\nBase SHA: `690f314ebb8602acc6736fa258ee7dba9d1b7930`\n\nReviewed commits in order:\n\n- `ea9c876` [task-1] Add foundation vocabulary files (DEEP-COMPONENTS.md, STACK.md)\n- `43d6810` [task-2] Add frontend depth gate 7 and renumber gates\n- `794e939` [task-3] Add core /frontend SKILL.md\n- `cb16105` [task-4] Add PATTERNS.md and TESTING.md\n- `4a2212c` [task-5] Add /ui-system skill with verbatim ShadCN/Radix guidelines\n- `ae379f3` [task-6] Add /react-perf skill with verbatim Vercel React best practices and web guidelines\n- `7b888ed` [task-7] Wire frontend skills into mission infrastructure\n- `cb32394` [task-8] Smoke test -- all structural checks pass\n- `dd6dd48` [task-9] Cleanup leftover test file and module shape audit\n- `9d2e32a` [task-10] Update docs -- README skills table, UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md gate refs, ARCH.md skill count\n- `398784f` chore: update progress and task status for mission completion\n\n## Findings By Commit\n\n### `ea9c876` [task-1] Foundation vocabulary files\n\n- No blocking findings. The deep-component rules match the glossary terms and the React guidance is broadly correct.\n\n### `43d6810` [task-2] Frontend depth gate 7\n\n- No blocking findings. Gate 7 is self-skipping, placed after module depth, and uses the same four rule names as `DEEP-COMPONENTS.md`.\n\n### `794e939` [task-3] Core `/frontend` skill\n\n- No blocking findings. The design-implement-test workflow matches `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md`, and the hook boundary is clear.\n\n### `cb16105` [task-4] PATTERNS.md and TESTING.md\n\n- No blocking findings. The testing guidance correctly favors rendered behavior, roles, labels, visible output, and user events over implementation details.\n\n### `4a2212c` [task-5] `/ui-system` skill\n\n- MEDIUM: `skills/ui-system/SKILL.md:104` makes `FieldGroup` + `Field` mandatory and says never use raw `div` with `grid gap-*` for form layout. That is too absolute for ShadCN projects because not every existing codebase has the newer Field components installed, and simple form grids are valid when they are already the local convention. In real missions this can push workers into adding or rewriting design-system components instead of matching the repo. Soften this to \"prefer existing Field components when present; otherwise match local form primitives.\"\n\n### `ae379f3` [task-6] `/react-perf` skill\n\n- MEDIUM: `skills/react-perf/SKILL.md:3841` says all interactive elements need keyboard handlers. That is false for native `<button>`, `<a>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, and other semantic controls, which already provide keyboard behavior. This can lead agents to add redundant or broken `onKeyDown` handlers. The rule should say custom non-native interactive elements need keyboard support, and semantic elements are preferred.\n\n- MEDIUM: `skills/react-perf/SKILL.md:3769` says `useEffectEvent` creates a stable function reference, but the same React guidance elsewhere says Effect Event function identity intentionally changes every render and must be omitted from dependency arrays. The practical subscription example is fine, but this sentence is factually wrong and will confuse workers reviewing dependencies. Replace \"stable function reference\" with \"non-reactive Effect Event that reads latest values and is omitted from effect dependencies.\"\n\n- MEDIUM: `skills/react-perf/SKILL.md:1` embeds a 4003-line skill entrypoint. Darkfactory skills are loaded as instructions, so this will flood the worker context even for small frontend tasks and mixes React perf, Next.js server patterns, JS micro-optimizations, and a UI review prompt in one file. Move the large Vercel material into reference files and keep `SKILL.md` as a short routing guide. Otherwise `/react-perf` becomes expensive and hard for agents to apply selectively.\n\n- LOW: `git diff --check 690f314ebb8602acc6736fa258ee7dba9d1b7930..HEAD` fails mostly on `skills/react-perf/SKILL.md` trailing whitespace. This is not behavior-breaking, but it contradicts the smoke-test claim that structural checks pass cleanly.\n\n### `7b888ed` [task-7] Mission infrastructure wiring\n\n- HIGH: `skills/mission-init/SKILL.md:125` requires checking whether `chrome-devtools-mcp` is registered, but lines 127-130 contain only comments and explicitly say the exact check depends on the registration mechanism. `skills/mission/SKILL.md:569` repeats the same vague \"check that chrome-devtools-mcp is available\" at dispatch time. This is not wired together correctly: future orchestrators cannot run a deterministic init or dispatch pre-flight, and frontend missions may either block incorrectly or skip visual verification without knowing. Add a concrete detection procedure for the actual Claude/Codex MCP environment, or mark the check as manual with exact evidence required.\n\n- MEDIUM: `skills/mission/templates/worker-brief.md:53` leaves both conditional methodology branches in the template text. Existing placeholders are normal in this template, but this section is easier to leak verbatim into a worker brief than the old single line was. If leaked, the worker sees `Use <Skill from task topology>` plus both `/frontend` and `/tdd` examples. Replace it with explicit placeholders such as `<methodology procedure line>` and `<constraint procedure lines>` so the generated brief contains only the selected methodology and constraints.\n\n### `cb32394` [task-8] Smoke test\n\n- CRITICAL: This commit adds the entire unrelated `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/` tree to the frontend mission diff. Examples include `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/proposal.md:1`, `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/tasks.md:1`, and `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/logs/adversarial-a-codex.log:1`. These files are unrelated to the frontend-architecture-skill mission and remain in the final `base..HEAD` diff. This blocks ship because it pollutes the PR with another mission's specs, logs, handoffs, and validations.\n\n- HIGH: The smoke-test commit and later progress claim \"all structural checks pass,\" but `git diff --check` fails on the final diff, including `openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/validations/task-7-scrutiny.md:3`, `skills/react-perf/SKILL.md:9`, and many more trailing-whitespace lines. If whitespace checks are not part of the intended structural checks, the validation should say so. If they are, the smoke test missed a deterministic failure.\n\n### `dd6dd48` [task-9] Cleanup + module shape audit\n\n- HIGH: The cleanup audit says \"No other unused files, dead references, or scaffolding found\" at `openspec/changes/frontend-architecture-skill/mission/validations/module-shape-audit.md:40`, but it did not remove the unrelated `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/` tree introduced by task 8. This is a direct cleanup miss and leaves the CRITICAL task-8 issue unresolved.\n\n### `9d2e32a` [task-10] Documentation updates\n\n- No new findings. README, ARCH, and glossary updates match the final skill count and the renumbered gate references.\n\n### `398784f` Mission completion chore\n\n- No new findings beyond carrying forward the unresolved task-8/task-9 blockers.\n\n## Overall Risk\n\n- The skills are mostly coherent and use the new terms consistently: Methodology, Constraint, Task layer, Browser tools, Frontend depth gate, Component contract, and Allowed-shallow exception.\n- The biggest real-world failure mode is operational: frontend visual verification depends on chrome-devtools-mcp, but the mission-init and dispatch flows do not define a runnable availability check.\n- The second biggest risk is review noise and accidental scope expansion: the final diff includes a complete unrelated OpenSpec change tree.\n\n## Verdict\n\nDo not ship as-is. Remove the unrelated `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/` additions, replace the browser-tools availability prose with an executable or explicitly manual check, and clean the trailing whitespace. The remaining skill-content issues are fixable follow-ups.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
